var e=[{week:1,unitId:`character`,title:`Volunteering — Time, Talent, and Energy`,focus:`Community contribution`,outcomeFocus:`Volunteering is donating time, talent, and energy to benefit people and community. It can build purpose and belonging.`,materials:[`Printed Week 1 worksheet or this page`,`Board: Time / Talent / Energy`,`Timer`],knowledge:[`Volunteering is donating time, talent, and energy to benefit people, communities, or the environment.`,`Volunteering is a choice to contribute — not a chore and not a punishment.`],understanding:[`Connecting knowledge and skills to volunteering can support personal and community well-being.`],skills:[`Connect knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities.`],warmUp:`Ask: “When has someone given their time to help you — a coach, a library volunteer, a lunch supervisor, a family member?” Two students share. Write Time, Talent, Energy on the board.`,teachTitle:`What volunteering is (and is not)`,teachSteps:[`Volunteering means you give time, talent, or energy to help people or a place — not because you are paid, but because it helps.`,`Time = hours you show up (shelving books, picking litter). Talent = a skill you share (reading with a buddy, drawing a poster). Energy = effort when the job is ordinary (stacking chairs, packing food hampers).`,`It is not the same as a chore you must do at home, and it is not a punishment. It is a choice to contribute.`,`Grade 5 examples that fit our community: school recycling team, reading buddy, helping at a community league event, packing kits for a food bank drive, walking dogs at a shelter with a family adult.`,`Helping can grow a sense of purpose (“I am useful here”) and belonging (“I am part of this group”). One regular, honest contribution is enough to start.`],story:`On a Thursday after school, Maya stayed to help Ms. Patel restock the lost-and-found. The bin smelled like wet mittens. Maya did not get paid. She folded hoodies, paired mittens, and printed a sign: Please take what is yours. Liam from Grade 3 found his toque and grinned like it was a gift.

Maya is not the loudest kid in the gym. She is good at noticing what is messy and making it neat. That is a talent. The time she gave was twenty minutes. The energy was the part where she kept folding even after her friends left for the bus loop.

The next morning the hallway felt calmer. Maya told the class she did not save the world — she just made the bin usable. Volunteering is a choice to contribute, not a chore and not a punishment. Maya sat a little taller. She had a place to be useful, and the Grade 3s had dry hats.`,activity:`Students complete the volunteer map. They do not need a real placement today — they connect skills they already have to a possible contribution. If a student says “I have no talents,” name one you have seen (holds the door, explains math, notices when someone is left out).`,worksheetTitle:`My volunteer map`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Volunteering = giving time, talent, or energy to help people or community.`},{kind:`text`,id:`skill`,label:`1. One skill or strength I already have`},{kind:`text`,id:`who`,label:`2. Who or what could that help? (school, family, community, land, animals)`},{kind:`table`,id:`idea`,columns:[`What I would do`,`Time, talent, or energy?`,`Who benefits`],rows:1},{kind:`text`,id:`barrier`,label:`4. One thing that might get in the way (transport, shyness, time) and one way around it`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`In one sentence: How could volunteering support your well-being or someone else’s?`}],sample:{skill:`I am good at organizing bins and making clear signs. I notice when the lost-and-found is a mess.`,who:`Our school hallway and younger students who lose mittens. Also the lunch supervisors who currently have to dig through the pile.`,idea:`Stay 20 minutes Thursdays to fold lost-and-found and make a claim-your-stuff sign | Time + talent (organizing) + energy | Grade 3s, office staff, anyone who lost a hat`,barrier:`I might miss the late bus. I can ask if I can start at 3:05 and still catch the 3:25 bus, or do it at lunch on Friday instead.`,exit:`Helping the lost-and-found makes me feel useful, and younger kids feel looked after when they get their hats back.`},checks:[`Can the student define volunteering in their own words (time, talent, or energy — not pay)?`,`Did they name one realistic Grade 5 contribution?`,`Can they say who would benefit?`],tip:`Keep ideas local and doable. Avoid “save the world” lists. School jobs count: recycling, younger-buddy reading, set-up crew. Never require unpaid labour as a grade.`,preview:`Next week we look at life experiences — the stories that shape how we see challenges.`},{week:2,unitId:`character`,title:`Life Experiences Shape Who We Are`,focus:`Experiences and understanding`,outcomeFocus:`Life experiences can inform strengths and preferences. They shape how we understand events and how we respond.`,materials:[`Week 2 worksheet`,`Board: Experience → What I learned → How I respond`],knowledge:[`Life experiences can be individual or shared.`,`Life experiences can inform strengths, skills, and preferences.`],understanding:[`Life experiences can shape an individual's understanding of events and how they respond.`],skills:[`Examine how life experiences can shape an individual's understanding of events.`],warmUp:`Read: “Two students both miss the same soccer goal. One laughs and tries again. One sits out.” Ask: What life experiences might sit behind those two responses? Take two ideas. No names, no roasting.`,teachTitle:`Experiences leave traces`,teachSteps:[`A life experience is something that happens to you or that you take part in: a move, a new school, a sport, a loss, a success, a family tradition, a tough test, a kind teacher.`,`Experiences can be individual (you practised piano alone) or shared (your class put on a concert).`,`Over time, experiences can teach skills, strengths, and preferences (“I like working with my hands”; “I need quiet before a test”).`,`They can also shape how we read a new situation. If you have been left out before, a group project can feel risky. If you have been coached well, a hard skill can feel possible.`],story:`Noah used to freeze during spelling bees at his old school in Sherwood Park. One year he blanked on a simple word and sat down shaking. At this school, students practise out loud with a partner before the Friday quiz. Noah still feels the old panic, but now he asks for the practise time instead of pretending he is fine.

When the class missed a wide shot in intramural soccer, two kids reacted differently. Noah laughed and asked for another try. A teammate sat out. Noah thought about the spelling bee. Missing once had taught him that a second try feels less huge if someone walks you through it.

He wrote on his private map: experience — freezing in a bee; skill — asking for practise; preference — a quiet rehearsal, not being put on the spot first. He did not share the bee story out loud. He only shared, I like a warm-up before I perform. That was enough. Everyday experiences count: a bee, a move, or learning to ride a bike on a gravel alley in Fort Saskatchewan.`,activity:`Students complete a private experience map. Tell them: you will not read these aloud. Share only if you choose the last box. Protect privacy — no forcing trauma stories. Everyday examples are enough (learning to ride a bike, joining choir, moving houses).`,worksheetTitle:`My experience map (private)`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Use school-appropriate examples. You do not have to write about anything painful. Everyday experiences count.`},{kind:`table`,id:`exp`,columns:[`The experience`,`Skill or strength it built`],rows:1},{kind:`text`,id:`pref`,label:`2. One experience that shaped a preference (how I like to learn, play, or rest)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`respond`,label:`3. How might that experience change the way I respond when something similar happens again?`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`share`,label:`4. Optional to share with a partner: one sentence I am willing to say out loud`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Finish: Life experiences can shape our understanding because ________.`}],sample:{exp:`Freezing in a spelling bee at my old school, then practising out loud here | Asking for a warm-up instead of pretending I am fine`,pref:`I like a quiet rehearsal with a partner before I have to perform in front of the class.`,respond:`If I feel panic before a quiz or a game, I will ask for practise time instead of shutting down or sitting out.`,share:`I like a warm-up before I perform.`,exit:`they teach us skills and how we read a new situation, like asking for practise after a freeze.`},checks:[`Did the student name an experience and a skill or preference that came from it?`,`Can they explain, in one line, that experiences influence how we respond?`,`Did they keep classmates’ stories private?`],tip:`Model a mild personal example first. If a student writes something that sounds like a safety disclosure, follow EIPS reporting — do not put it under the document camera.`,preview:`Next week: perseverance — staying with hard things without fake cheerfulness.`},{week:3,unitId:`character`,title:`Perseverance — Staying With Hard Things`,focus:`Effort and well-being`,outcomeFocus:`Perseverance is finding ways to continue to improve skills and stay motivated during difficult situations. It includes effort, courage, commitment, and belief.`,materials:[`Week 3 worksheet`,`Board: Effort · Courage · Commitment · Belief`],knowledge:[`Perseverance is finding ways to continue to improve skills and stay motivated during difficult situations.`,`Perseverance includes effort, courage, commitment, and belief.`],understanding:[`Perseverance can lead to positive feelings associated with achieving a goal, for individuals and communities. Rest is part of the plan, not quitting.`],skills:[`Describe how perseverance can affect well-being.`],warmUp:`Show a 10-second “first try vs later try” story (tying shoes, a math strategy, a jump rope trick). Ask: What did the person do between try one and try ten? List verbs: practised, asked for help, rested, tried a new way.`,teachTitle:`Name perseverance without fake cheerfulness`,teachSteps:[`Perseverance is not “never feel stuck.” It is staying with a hard thing long enough to improve — sometimes by changing the plan.`,`Four supports: effort (you show up), courage (you try while nervous), commitment (you return after a break), belief (you think improvement is possible).`,`Perseverance can be individual (you keep decoding a book) or group/community (a class fundraiser that takes weeks).`,`It can lead to positive feelings when a goal moves forward. It can also be tiring. Rest is part of the plan, not quitting.`],story:`Aisha’s recorder squeaked on the high B. Band was in twelve minutes. She wanted to shove the instrument in her backpack and claim a sore throat. Instead she opened her case, played the bar slowly, and asked Mr. Kwan if she could sit in the second row today so she could watch his fingers.

Effort looked like six extra minutes at lunch. Courage looked like playing the squeaky note in front of two friends. Commitment looked like going home, resting her jaw, and coming back Thursday. Belief looked like writing in her agenda: High B gets cleaner if I slow down.

By Friday the note was not perfect. It was less of a duck. Aisha did not make a hero speech. She told the class perseverance is staying with a hard thing long enough to improve — sometimes by changing the plan, not by pretending it is easy. Rest, she added, is part of the plan. Her protective factor was Mr. Kwan’s fingering chart taped inside the case.`,activity:`Students pick one real hard thing from school, sport, music, friendship, or a chore. They map what perseverance could look like this week — not a hero speech.`,worksheetTitle:`Perseverance plan (one hard thing)`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`hard`,label:`The hard thing (school-appropriate)`},{kind:`table`,id:`supports`,columns:[`Support`,`What this could look like for me this week`],rowLabels:[`Effort`,`Courage`,`Commitment (including rest)`,`Belief I can improve`]},{kind:`text`,id:`help`,label:`One person or tool that could help (not “try harder”)`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How could perseverance on this thing support your well-being? (mood, confidence, health, belonging)`}],sample:{hard:`Getting a clean high B on recorder before band on Fridays.`,supports:`Effort: six extra minutes at lunch with the fingering chart. | Courage: play the squeaky note in front of two friends. | Commitment: rest my jaw, come back Thursday. | Belief: High B gets cleaner if I slow down.`,help:`Mr. Kwan’s fingering chart taped inside the case, and sitting in the second row to watch his fingers.`,exit:`I dread band less, I feel more able, and I still belong in the group even when the note is not perfect yet.`},checks:[`Can the student tell perseverance from “pretend it is easy”?`,`Did they name a concrete action, not only “don’t give up”?`,`Did they include a help source or a rest plan?`],tip:`Watch for students who confuse perseverance with never asking for help. Praise “I tried a new strategy” more than “I suffered longer.”`,preview:`Next week we connect skills to volunteering and community.`},{week:4,unitId:`character`,title:`Strengths, Skills, and Community Contribution`,focus:`From me to we`,outcomeFocus:`Every individual has the ability to contribute in different ways. Connect personal knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities.`,materials:[`Week 4 worksheet`,`Optional: list of real school volunteer jobs`],knowledge:[`Communities need many kinds of contribution, not only loud leadership.`,`Life experiences are clues to where a skill can help.`],understanding:[`Connecting knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities can support personal and community well-being.`],skills:[`Connect knowledge and skills to volunteering opportunities.`],warmUp:`Rapid list on the board: quiet strengths (notices litter, remembers names, good with younger kids, patient with tech). Students add two more. Point out: loud leadership is only one kind.`,teachTitle:`Match skills to a real need`,teachSteps:[`Communities need many kinds of contribution: organizers, greeters, lifters, artists, translators, people who stay after to put things away.`,`Your life experiences (Weeks 1–3) are clues. If you have moved schools, you know how a new student feels. If you play an instrument, you can help at a concert set-up.`,`A good match is useful and realistic. “Become famous and donate millions” is not a Grade 5 plan. “Help the office stuff envelopes on Friday” is.`,`Contribution can be short (one event) or ongoing (a weekly job). Both count.`],story:`Liam is not the kid who grabs the microphone on assembly day. He remembers names. When a new student, Priya, arrived from another Elk Island school, Liam showed her the boot rack and the quiet side of the playground.

The class matched skills to real needs. Liam wrote: skill — remembering names and noticing who is left out; need — new students feeling lost in a big building; idea — a Friday welcome walk with the office’s permission.

The first Friday felt awkward. He almost hid in the library. Then he thought of last week — commitment includes coming back after a wobble. He asked Ms. Patel if he could be on the new-kid tour crew with one other student. She said yes, twenty minutes, once a week if needed. Loud leadership is only one kind. Greeters, translators, people who put chairs away — the school needs those too.`,activity:`Students complete a match-up table: three skills, three possible contributions, one they could actually try at school this month. If your school has real jobs, post them.`,worksheetTitle:`Skill-to-contribution match`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`match`,columns:[`A skill or knowledge I have`,`A community need it could help`,`School or community idea`],rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`try`,label:`My realistic try this month (one idea, with when and who I would ask)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`link`,label:`How this links to perseverance (what might be awkward at first)`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Complete: I can contribute by _____ because I _____.`}],sample:{match:`Remembering names and noticing who is left out | New students feeling lost | Friday welcome walk with the office`,try:`Ask Ms. Patel this Friday if I can join the new-kid tour crew, twenty minutes, with one other student.`,link:`The first time will feel awkward. Commitment includes coming back after a wobble.`,exit:`I can contribute by greeting new students because I remember names and I know how a new building feels.`},checks:[`Is the try realistic for Grade 5 (time, permission, local)?`,`Did they connect a real skill, not a superhero identity?`],tip:`If a student cannot volunteer outside school, school jobs still count. Do not grade the size of the project.`,preview:`Next week: protective factors — the supports that help us keep going.`},{week:5,unitId:`character`,title:`Protective Factors That Help Us Keep Going`,focus:`Supports for perseverance`,outcomeFocus:`Protective factors are conditions or attributes in individuals, families, and communities that support well-being and help people persevere.`,materials:[`Week 5 worksheet`,`Board: People · Places · Skills · Routines`],knowledge:[`Protective factors support perseverance and well-being (supportive relationships, problem-solving, sense of purpose, belonging, rest, asking for help).`,`Protective factors can be people, places, skills, or routines.`],understanding:[`Perseverance is more likely when protective factors are in place — it is not only willpower.`],skills:[`Identify protective factors that support perseverance.`],warmUp:`Ask: “When you have a hard week, what actually helps — not what people say should help?” Collect four answers. Sort them into People, Places, Skills, Routines.`,teachTitle:`Willpower is not the whole story`,teachSteps:[`A protective factor is something that makes it more likely you can keep going and stay well: a person who listens, a place that feels safe, a skill you can use, a routine that steadies you.`,`People: a coach, a cousin, a teacher, a neighbour, a classmate who saves you a seat.`,`Places: the library nook, a community league rink, grandma’s kitchen, the art room at lunch.`,`Skills: asking for a break, breaking a task into steps, naming a feeling, using the fingering chart.`,`Routines: sleep, water bottle, practise after snack, Friday check-in with a trusted adult.`,`Protective factors do not erase hard things. They make staying with the hard thing more possible.`],story:`Jordan’s math unit felt like a wall. He almost decided he was “just bad at it.” Then he listed what actually helped last month: Ms. Grant’s after-school table on Tuesdays (a person and a place), the strategy of doing the first two questions only and then raising a hand (a skill), and going to bed before the late hockey recap (a routine).

He crossed out “try harder” as a plan. Trying harder with no sleep had already failed. His protective-factor card named one to strengthen: ask his uncle, who likes puzzles, to sit with him for ten minutes on Thursday. That is not cheating. That is a factor.

The class agreed: perseverance without protective factors is just grinding. With them, you have a way through.`,activity:`Students map protective factors they already have and one they could strengthen this month. Private is allowed. Everyday is enough.`,worksheetTitle:`My protective-factor card`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`factors`,columns:[`Kind`,`One I already have`,`How it helps me keep going`],rowLabels:[`Person`,`Place`,`Skill`,`Routine`]},{kind:`text`,id:`strengthen`,label:`One protective factor I could strengthen this month (small and realistic)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why is “just try harder” a weak plan compared with naming a protective factor?`}],sample:{factors:`Person: Ms. Grant’s Tuesday table. Place: library nook. Skill: first two questions then hand up. Routine: sleep before the recap.`,strengthen:`Ask my uncle to sit with me for ten minutes on Thursday while I start the review sheet.`,exit:`Trying harder with no sleep already failed. A person, place, skill, or routine gives me a way through — not just more grinding.`},checks:[`Did they name factors in more than one category?`,`Is the “strengthen” item small enough to actually try?`],tip:`Do not force family details. School adults and school places are valid protective factors. If a student cannot name a person, help them name one at school.`,preview:`Next week we wrap the unit: relating life experiences to well-being.`},{week:6,unitId:`character`,title:`Relating Life Experiences to Well-Being`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Pull the thread: volunteering, experiences, perseverance, contribution, and protective factors all relate to well-being.`,materials:[`Week 6 worksheet`,`Unit rubric (teacher)`],knowledge:[`Well-being includes feeling able, belonging, and having purpose — not only “being happy.”`],understanding:[`Life experiences, perseverance, and contribution can support well-being when protective factors are in place.`],skills:[`Reflect and relate life experiences to perseverance and well-being.`,`Describe how perseverance can affect well-being.`],warmUp:`Board: purpose · belonging · confidence · mood · health. Ask: which of these can volunteering or perseverance touch? Take two examples from earlier weeks (lost-and-found, recorder, welcome walk).`,teachTitle:`Tell the whole story in one page`,teachSteps:[`This unit’s outcome: reflect and relate life experiences to perseverance and well-being.`,`A strong wrap does four jobs: names an experience, names a perseverance move, names a contribution or skill, and names an effect on well-being.`,`Well-being is not a smiley-face contest. “I dread band less” counts. “Younger kids got their hats back” counts.`,`Use the rubric once near the end of the six weeks. Worksheets stay formative.`],story:`The class laid six weeks on the table. Maya’s lost-and-found. Noah asking for a warm-up. Aisha’s high B. Liam’s welcome walk. Jordan’s Tuesday math table.

Nobody had saved the world. Each person could finish this sentence: Because of ____, I practised perseverance by ____, and that helped well-being because ____.

Jordan wrote: Because math felt like a wall, I practised perseverance by using the first-two-questions skill and a Tuesday table, and that helped well-being because I stopped calling myself bad at it and I showed up. That is relating a life experience to perseverance and well-being. That is the outcome.`,activity:`Students write a short wrap using the four-part frame. Optional share of the last sentence only. Then a quick self-check against the rubric (proficient is the target).`,worksheetTitle:`Relating the pieces`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`exp`,label:`One life experience from this unit or from my year (everyday is enough)`},{kind:`text`,id:`pers`,label:`How I practised perseverance (effort, courage, commitment, belief, or a new plan)`},{kind:`text`,id:`give`,label:`A skill I can contribute, or a volunteer idea I still think is realistic`},{kind:`text`,id:`protect`,label:`One protective factor I will keep using`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Because of ____, I practised perseverance by ____, and that helped well-being because ____.`}],sample:{exp:`Math this month felt like a wall.`,pers:`I used the first-two-questions skill and went to the Tuesday table. Rest on Wednesday so I could come back Thursday.`,give:`I can help a younger student who is stuck on a first question, because I know how that freeze feels.`,protect:`Tuesday after-school table and sleep before late recaps.`,exit:`Because math felt like a wall, I practised perseverance by using a skill and a person, and that helped well-being because I stopped calling myself bad at it and I showed up.`},checks:[`Does the exit sentence include experience, perseverance, and well-being?`,`Did they avoid a hero speech?`],tip:`Score the unit rubric once now or next class. Do not score every worksheet out of 4. Proficient is the target.`,preview:`Weeks 7–12: Healthy Relationships — perspectives, listening, communication.`}],t=[{week:7,unitId:`relationships`,title:`How Experiences Shape Perspectives`,focus:`What a perspective is`,outcomeFocus:`A perspective is how someone understands, thinks, and feels about a situation. Experiences, personal factors, and environment can shape it.`,materials:[`Week 7 worksheet`,`Simple scenario cards (on the worksheet)`],knowledge:[`A perspective is how someone understands, thinks, and feels about a situation.`,`Experiences, personal factors, and environment can influence perspective.`],understanding:[`Two people can both be honest and still see a situation differently.`],skills:[`Describe how experiences can affect perspectives of self and others.`],warmUp:`Hold up an object (mug, ball). Students on the left describe only what they see; students on the right do the same. Point out: same object, different view. That is a tiny model of perspective.`,teachTitle:`Perspective is not the same as “right vs wrong”`,teachSteps:[`Perspective = how a person understands a situation from where they stand — their experiences, feelings, and information.`,`Two people can both be honest and still see a recess problem differently.`,`Factors that influence perspective: past experiences, culture and family, how tired or hungry you are, where you were standing, what you heard first.`,`We acknowledge a perspective when we can say it fairly, even if we do not agree.`],story:`A chipped Elk Island mug. Students on the window side described the handle. Students on the door side described a crack they could see and the others could not. Same mug, different view.

Then Scenario A. Sam had the soccer ball. Riley said Sam always hogged it last year. Sam had just joined the school this month and did not know about the rotation. Elena wrote Sam’s perspective without picking a winner: Sam thinks it is an open game; nobody told them the rule. Mateo wrote Riley’s: last year felt unfair, so this year already looks like the same pattern.

Nobody in the class crowned a hero. Perspective is how a person understands a situation from where they stand. Two people can both be honest. Acknowledging a perspective means saying it fairly even if you do not agree yet.`,activity:`Students read two short recess scenarios and write each person’s likely perspective. No picking a winner yet — that is a later week.`,worksheetTitle:`Two perspectives`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Scenario A. Sam used the soccer ball at recess. Riley has been waiting and says Sam always hogged it last year. Sam just joined the school this month and did not know there was a rotation.`},{kind:`table`,id:`a`,columns:[`Person`,`What they might think / feel`,`What experience might sit behind that`],rowLabels:[`Sam`,`Riley`]},{kind:`intro`,text:`Scenario B. The teacher asks the class to move seats. Kai is glad (talking neighbours). Morgan is upset (liked the quiet corner).`},{kind:`table`,id:`b`,columns:[`Person`,`Perspective in one sentence`],rowLabels:[`Kai`,`Morgan`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Name one personal or environmental factor that can change a perspective.`}],sample:{a:`Sam: This is an open game. Nobody told me a rule. Just joined this month. | Riley: Last year felt hogged, so this year already looks unfair.`,b:`Kai: I am glad because I like talking neighbours. | Morgan: I am upset because the quiet corner helped me work.`,exit:`Where you were standing, what you heard first, or how tired you are — like being stuck as goalie the whole recess.`},checks:[`Can they define perspective without using “they’re wrong”?`,`Did they write both sides fairly in Scenario A?`,`Can they name a factor (experience, place, information)?`],tip:`Stop any “Riley is just dramatic” talk. The skill is fair restating. You can disagree later; you cannot skip understanding.`,preview:`Next week we listen so others feel heard.`},{week:8,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Listening So Others Feel Heard`,focus:`Listening skills`,outcomeFocus:`Listening skills that support understanding another person’s perspective — not listening to reload your own point.`,materials:[`Week 8 worksheet`,`Partner seating`],knowledge:[`Listening skills include paying attention, waiting, paraphrasing, and asking clarifying questions.`],understanding:[`Perspectives can be acknowledged when people listen to understand, not to reload.`],skills:[`Apply listening skills to understand another person's perspective.`],warmUp:`Model two 20-second listens. First: you interrupt, look away, and say “same, but me.” Second: you face the speaker, wait, and paraphrase. Ask which one made the speaker feel heard.`,teachTitle:`Four listening moves`,teachSteps:[`Body: face the person, devices down, don’t fake-listen while scanning the room.`,`Wait: let them finish. Silence of two seconds is allowed.`,`Paraphrase: “So you felt ___ because ___.” Check: “Did I get that right?”`,`Curious question: one question that is not a trap. “What happened next?” beats “Why would you do that?”`],story:`Harper’s partner, Dev, talked for forty-five seconds about a time the art supplies vanished before his group could start. Harper’s first instinct was to jump in with her own story about missing markers. She bit that back. She faced Dev, waited two seconds after he finished, and tried a paraphrase: So you felt rushed because the bin was empty and you thought we didn’t save you anything?

Dev said, Partly. I also thought the teacher would blame our group. Harper asked one curious question that was not a trap: What happened next? not Why didn’t you just grab them earlier?

They swapped. On the worksheet Harper checked You heard me: partly, because Dev had skipped the part about her stomach dropping. They tried once more. The second paraphrase landed. Coaching line on the board: Say their view, not your verdict. Waiting was the hardest move. Silence felt like failing. It was actually the work.`,activity:`Pairs: Speaker has 45 seconds on “a time something felt unfair at school (no names).” Listener uses the four moves, then writes the paraphrase. Swap. If odd number, a triad with an observer.`,worksheetTitle:`Listen and paraphrase`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Speaker topic: a school-appropriate time something felt unfair (no classmate names).`},{kind:`text`,id:`para`,label:`Their perspective in my words`,rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`q`,label:`The question I asked`},{kind:`choice`,id:`interrupt`,label:`Did I interrupt?`,options:[`Yes`,`No`]},{kind:`choice`,id:`look`,label:`Did I look at them?`,options:[`Yes`,`No`]},{kind:`choice`,id:`heard`,label:`Partner’s check: “You heard me.”`,options:[`Yes`,`Partly`,`Not yet`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Which listening move is hardest for you, and why?`}],sample:{para:`So you felt rushed because the art bin was empty and you thought our group would get blamed for not starting. Did I get that right?`,q:`What happened next? (not a trap question)`,interrupt:`No`,look:`Yes`,heard:`Partly`,exit:`Waiting. Silence feels like I am failing, but it is actually the work so I do not reload my own story.`},checks:[`Did the paraphrase match what the speaker meant?`,`Was there one curious question that was not an attack?`,`Can they name a listening move they still need?`],tip:`Sit where you can hear interruptions. Praise the paraphrase, not the drama of the story. Coaching sentence: “Say their view, not your verdict.”`,preview:`Next week: communicating with respect — fact, effect, ask.`},{week:9,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Communicating With Respect`,focus:`Clear, kind, specific`,outcomeFocus:`Respectful communication skills — I-messages, specific requests, tone and body language that match the words.`,materials:[`Week 9 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Communication skills include using respectful language, tone, and body language; being specific; using I-messages.`],understanding:[`A specific ask works better than a put-down because the other person can actually do the thing.`],skills:[`Apply communication skills to connect perspectives respectfully.`],warmUp:`Read two lines in a flat voice then a sharp voice: “I wasn’t finished with that.” Same words, different impact. Students vote: which one invites a conversation?`,teachTitle:`A respectful message has three parts`,teachSteps:[`What I noticed (fact): “The markers were dumped in the bin.”`,`How it affected me (feeling or need): “I couldn’t find a working one.”`,`A clear ask: “Please put lids on and stand them up.”`,`Avoid you-always attacks, sarcasm, and public call-outs. Respect includes volume, timing (not in front of a crowd if it can wait), and accepting that the other person may need a minute.`],story:`Someone dumped markers in the bin with no lids. Dev’s blunt line in his head was, You always wreck our group. He practised the three-part message instead, first in a flat voice, then in a sharp one. Same words, different impact. The class voted: the flat, specific version invited a conversation.

Dev wrote: I noticed the markers were dumped without lids. It affected me because I could not find a working one for the map labels. Could you put lids on and stand them up? He crossed out thief and stupid idea on the rewrite table.

A specific ask works better than a put-down because the other person can actually do the thing. Stop being awful is not a job. Please return the eraser after math is.`,activity:`Rewrite three blunt lines into respectful three-part messages. Then write one real line they might need this week (optional to use in real life).`,worksheetTitle:`Rewrite for respect`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Change each blunt line into: I noticed… It affected me… Could you…`},{kind:`table`,id:`rewrite`,columns:[`Blunt line`,`Respectful three-part message`],rowLabels:[`You always wreck our group.`,`Give it back, thief.`,`That’s a stupid idea.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`real`,label:`One real situation I might need this for (no names required)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does a specific ask work better than a put-down?`}],sample:{rewrite:`I noticed the markers were dumped without lids. It affected me because I could not find a working one. Could you put lids on and stand them up? | I noticed my eraser did not come back after math. Could you return it at recess? | I noticed a different plan. Could we try yours for five minutes and then check?`,real:`A borrowed eraser that never came back. Ask at recess, not during the mini-lesson.`,exit:`The other person can actually do the thing. Stop being awful is not a job. Please return the eraser after math is.`},checks:[`Do the rewrites include a fact, an effect, and an ask?`,`Did they drop sarcasm?`,`Can they explain why timing and tone matter?`],tip:`Collect one strong rewrite under the document camera with no student names on it.`,preview:`Next week: when perspectives differ — shared understanding, not a winner.`},{week:10,unitId:`relationships`,title:`When Perspectives Differ`,focus:`Shared understanding`,outcomeFocus:`Shared understanding is not the same as full agreement. It means both people can say the other view fairly and name a next step.`,materials:[`Week 10 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Shared understanding includes restating both sides, naming overlap, and agreeing on a next step even if the whole story is not settled.`],understanding:[`Healthy relationships are supported when perspectives are considered, even when people still disagree.`],skills:[`Work cooperatively to reach a shared understanding.`],warmUp:`Return to Sam and Riley’s soccer ball. Ask: if both perspectives are honest, what is a next step that does not pick a villain?`,teachTitle:`Three moves toward shared understanding`,teachSteps:[`Restate both views fairly (Week 7 skill).`,`Name overlap: both want a fair game; both want to play.`,`Agree on a next step: a rotation, a timer, a third person as referee for one recess — not a courtroom.`,`You can still disagree about last year. You do not have to solve the whole history in 12 minutes.`],story:`Riley still felt last year in the chest. Sam still felt accused for a rule nobody had explained. The class did not vote on a winner. They wrote both views, then the overlap: both want a fair game and both want to play.

Next step: a three-touch rotation for the rest of the week, posted on the door, and a check-in Friday. Not a punishment. A shared plan.

Shared understanding, the class said, is when each person can say the other view without twisting it, and both can live with the next step. It is not “I won the argument.”`,activity:`Students take one scenario (soccer, seats, or a group-project idea clash) and complete: both views, overlap, next step.`,worksheetTitle:`Shared understanding, not a winner`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`scene`,label:`The situation (no real classmate names)`},{kind:`text`,id:`view1`,label:`Person A’s view, said fairly`},{kind:`text`,id:`view2`,label:`Person B’s view, said fairly`},{kind:`text`,id:`overlap`,label:`Overlap (what both people want or need)`},{kind:`text`,id:`next`,label:`A next step both could live with`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How is shared understanding different from winning an argument?`}],sample:{scene:`Soccer ball at recess — rotation not explained to a new student.`,view1:`Sam thinks it is an open game and feels accused.`,view2:`Riley remembers last year and already feels left out.`,overlap:`Both want a fair game and both want to play.`,next:`Three-touch rotation for the rest of the week, posted on the door, check-in Friday.`,exit:`Each person can say the other view without twisting it, and both can live with the next step. Nobody has to be the villain.`},checks:[`Are both views fair, not sarcastic?`,`Is the next step doable this week?`],tip:`If a real recess conflict is live, do not put those two students on the spot. Use the worksheet scenario.`,preview:`Next week we practise the skills on a shared task.`},{week:11,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Working Together on a Shared Task`,focus:`Practise the skills`,outcomeFocus:`Apply listening, respectful talk, and shared understanding while actually working together.`,materials:[`Week 11 worksheet`,`One shared task (booth plan, classroom job roster, or recess-game rules)`],knowledge:[`Respectful collaboration includes listening, specific communication, and sharing the work.`],understanding:[`Skills only count if they show up when the work is real.`],skills:[`Demonstrate respectful communication while working on a shared task.`],warmUp:`Pairs have 60 seconds to agree on a team name for a pretend class fair booth. Then: who talked more? Who asked a question?`,teachTitle:`The task is the practice`,teachSteps:[`Today’s job is small and real: plan a class fair booth in 12 minutes (name, two jobs, one material list).`,`Use the four listening moves and the three-part message if you get stuck.`,`Shared understanding: if you disagree, restate, find overlap, pick a next step.`,`Everyone writes. Nobody is only the boss or only the scribe unless you rotate.`],story:`Harper wanted a popcorn booth. Dev wanted a bookmark stall because popcorn meant a hot plate and a supervisor. They almost defaulted to “fine, whatever.” Then Harper paraphrased: So you are worried about safety and an adult, not about bookmarks being cooler. Dev said yes. Overlap: both wanted a booth kids would actually visit that a Grade 5 could run.

Next step: a bookmark-and-sticker stall with a popcorn-themed design, no hot plate. They split jobs: Harper materials, Dev sign, both names on the plan. The work was ordinary. The skill was the point.`,activity:`Pairs or triads complete a booth (or classroom job) plan. Each person fills the process side of the worksheet: one listen, one respectful line, one compromise.`,worksheetTitle:`Shared task — class fair booth`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`booth`,label:`Booth name and what we will offer`},{kind:`table`,id:`jobs`,columns:[`Job`,`Who`],rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`listen`,label:`One time I paraphrased or asked a curious question`},{kind:`text`,id:`talk`,label:`One respectful three-part line we needed (or could have used)`},{kind:`text`,id:`share`,label:`Where our perspectives differed and what we did`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Which relationship skill showed up in the work, not just on the page?`}],sample:{booth:`Bookmark-and-sticker stall with a popcorn-themed design. No hot plate.`,jobs:`Materials — Harper | Sign — Dev | Both names on the plan`,listen:`So you are worried about safety and an adult, not about bookmarks being cooler.`,talk:`I noticed popcorn needs a hot plate. It affects whether a supervisor has to stand there. Could we keep the theme without cooking?`,share:`Popcorn vs bookmarks. Overlap: a booth kids visit that Grade 5 can run. Next step: theme without the hot plate.`,exit:`Paraphrase. We almost said fine, whatever. Restating the worry saved the plan.`},checks:[`Did more than one person have a real job?`,`Is there evidence of listening or a respectful ask, not only a pretty booth name?`],tip:`If a pair finishes in four minutes, they are skipping the process. Sit beside them and ask for the paraphrase out loud.`,preview:`Next week: unit wrap — connecting perspectives to healthy relationships.`},{week:12,unitId:`relationships`,title:`Connecting Perspectives to Healthy Relationships`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Healthy relationships are supported when we acknowledge perspectives, listen, communicate with respect, and work toward shared understanding.`,materials:[`Week 12 worksheet`,`Unit rubric`],knowledge:[`Healthy relationships are built on communication, collaboration, empathy, and respect.`],understanding:[`Perspectives of self and others can be acknowledged and connected through communication and listening.`],skills:[`Acknowledge and connect perspectives of self and others through communication and listening skills.`],warmUp:`Four corners of the board: perspective, listen, respectful talk, shared understanding. Students put one sticky (or one spoken line) on the skill they used most this unit and one they still need.`,teachTitle:`Name the outcome in kid language`,teachSteps:[`The outcome: acknowledge and connect perspectives of self and others through communication and listening skills.`,`Acknowledge = say it fairly. Connect = find overlap and a next step. Communication and listening = the tools.`,`A healthy relationship is not “we never disagree.” It is “we can disagree without erasing each other.”`,`Score the unit rubric once. Proficient is the target.`],story:`The soccer ball, the art bin, the borrowed eraser, the fair booth. None of those stories needed a villain. They needed someone to say the other view fairly, wait two seconds, and ask for a specific next step.

Elena wrote the kid-language outcome: I can say someone else’s view without twisting it, and I can say mine without attacking, and we can pick a next step. That is connecting perspectives. That is the relationship.`,activity:`Students complete a four-skill wrap and one “I still need” goal. Optional: one sentence they are willing to use this week at recess.`,worksheetTitle:`Relationship wrap`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`skills`,columns:[`Skill`,`One example from this unit (or from real life, no names)`],rowLabels:[`Perspective (say it fairly)`,`Listening`,`Respectful talk`,`Shared understanding`]},{kind:`text`,id:`need`,label:`The skill I still need, and one way I will practise it`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`In kid language: what does it mean to connect perspectives?`}],sample:{skills:`Sam/Riley soccer — both honest. | Paraphrase of the empty art bin. | I-message about lids. | Three-touch rotation as a next step.`,need:`Waiting. I will count two seconds before I jump in with my story.`,exit:`I can say someone else’s view without twisting it, say mine without attacking, and pick a next step together.`},checks:[`Can they state the outcome without the word “winning”?`,`Did they name a skill they still need?`],tip:`Use the rubric now. Do not average six worksheets. Look at the wrap plus one strong earlier sheet.`,preview:`Weeks 13–18: Safety — responsibility, privacy, consent and refusal.`}],n=[{week:13,unitId:`safety`,title:`Responsibility and the Impact of Choices`,focus:`Actions have consequences`,outcomeFocus:`Responsibility means owning a choice, naming who it affects, and repairing if needed.`,materials:[`Week 13 worksheet`,`Timer`],knowledge:[`Responsibility includes accepting the results of one's actions.`,`Impacts can be physical, social-emotional, or practical (time, money, trust).`],understanding:[`Actions have consequences that can affect the well-being of self and others.`],skills:[`Analyze the impact of actions on the well-being of self and others.`],warmUp:`Chain: a backpack in the aisle. Who besides the owner is affected? List three impacts.`,teachTitle:`Responsibility is not the same as blame`,teachSteps:[`Responsible = I own my choice, I can name who it affects, I can repair.`,`Impacts can be physical, social-emotional, or practical (time, money, trust).`,`Factors: hurry, audience, not knowing the rule, mood, trying to be funny.`,`An apology without a changed next choice is incomplete.`],story:`Owen left his backpack in the aisle for a second. Three people were affected: Ms. Patel nearly tripped, the class lost a minute, and Owen’s water bottle rolled under a desk and leaked on someone’s duo-tang.

Responsibility is not a shame pile-on. Owen owned the choice, named the impacts — physical, almost a fall; practical, time and a wet booklet; social, the owner of the duo-tang felt dismissed — and repaired. He picked up the bag, wiped the floor, and offered to recopy the ruined page at lunch.

They traced hypotheticals, not real class incidents. Forwarding a screenshot of a private message: trust is affected; repair is delete, apologize, and not do it again. An apology without a changed next choice is incomplete.`,activity:`Trace three scenarios: choice → who is affected → responsible next step. No real class incidents as examples.`,worksheetTitle:`Choice → impact → repair`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`trace`,columns:[`Situation`,`Who could be affected?`,`Responsible next step`],rowLabels:[`You forward a screenshot of a classmate's private message.`,`You sit at the nut-aware table with a granola bar.`,`You said you would walk a younger buddy home, then went to the park.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`real`,label:`One real choice I made this month (small is fine) and who it affected`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Responsibility includes accepting results. What does that mean in your own words?`}],sample:{trace:`Classmate (trust), anyone who sees it, you — delete, apologize, do not repeat. | Allergic classmate, supervisors — move, tell an adult, wash hands. | Buddy, family, teacher — show up or get an adult to cover; apologize.`,real:`I left my backpack in the aisle. Ms. Patel nearly tripped, the class lost a minute, a duo-tang got wet. I picked up the bag, wiped the floor, and recopied the page.`,exit:`I own the choice, I name who it affected, and I repair. An apology without a changed next choice is incomplete.`},checks:[`Named an impact on someone else, not only “I'd get in trouble”?`,`Included repair, not only the word sorry?`],tip:`Do not use a real class incident. Hypotheticals keep dignity intact.`,preview:`Next week: laws, rules, practices, and protocols — why the structures exist.`},{week:14,unitId:`safety`,title:`Laws, Rules, Practices, and Protocols`,focus:`Why the structures exist`,outcomeFocus:`Identify laws, rules, practices, and protocols that support safety and well-being.`,materials:[`Week 14 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Laws, rules, practices, and protocols support safety and well-being.`,`A protocol is a sequence of steps for a specific situation. A rule is a boundary.`],understanding:[`These structures protect people. They work only if people follow them.`],skills:[`Identify laws, rules, practices, and protocols that support safety.`],warmUp:`Students guess the difference between a law, a school rule, a practice, and a protocol before you teach it.`,teachTitle:`Four layers`,teachSteps:[`Law: whole country/province (seatbelts; it is against the law to threaten someone).`,`School rule: our building (phones away, hall passes).`,`Practice: a habit when no one is watching (walk on the right, wash hands).`,`Protocol: agreed steps for a specific situation (fire drill: line, silent, attendance clipboard).`,`If a rule is confusing, ask — do not skip it.`],story:`Fatima guessed that protocol meant being polite. Close, but not it. Four layers: a law, like seatbelts; a school rule, like phones in lockers; a practice, like walking on the right when no one is watching; and a protocol, the agreed steps for a specific situation.

They walked a fire drill in words: silent line, attendance clipboard, wait for all-clear. Who it protects: every person in the building, including visitors and the student who is in the bathroom. If only some people follow it, the attendance count is wrong and someone might be left in a hallway.

Lockdown she labelled protocol, not a scary story. Lights off, out of sight, silent, wait for officials. The point was the steps, said calmly. A protocol is a sequence. A rule is a boundary. Both protect people, but a protocol tells your feet what to do in order when your brain is noisy.`,activity:`Classify examples, then write one school protocol in order.`,worksheetTitle:`Law, rule, practice, protocol`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Label: L = law, R = school rule, P = practice, O = protocol.`},{kind:`table`,id:`class`,columns:[`Example`,`L / R / P / O`,`Who does it protect?`],rowLabels:[`Fire drill: silent line, attendance, wait for all-clear`,`Wear a seatbelt`,`Phones in lockers during class`,`Wash hands before eating`,`Lockdown: lights off, out of sight, silent, wait for officials`]},{kind:`text`,id:`steps`,label:`One protocol we use — steps in order`,rows:3},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What goes wrong if only some people follow a protocol?`}],sample:{class:`O — everyone in the building. | L — drivers and passengers. | R — learning time and privacy. | P — you and people who share tables. | O — everyone until officials say it is clear.`,steps:`Fire drill: silent line, teacher takes the attendance clipboard, walk to the meeting spot, wait for all-clear, then return.`,exit:`The attendance count is wrong and someone might be left in a hallway. A protocol only works if the steps happen in order for everyone.`},checks:[`Can they tell a protocol (steps) from a general rule?`,`Did they name who is protected?`],tip:`Stay calm on lockdown. This is not a fear lesson. Anxious students: follow up privately.`,preview:`Next week: personal safety and trusted adults.`},{week:15,unitId:`safety`,title:`Personal Safety and Trusted Adults`,focus:`Safe, unsafe, uncomfortable`,outcomeFocus:`Personal safety includes freedom from physical harm, illness, and emotional harm. Name trusted adults.`,materials:[`Week 15 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Personal safety includes freedom from physical harm, illness, and emotional harm.`,`A trusted adult listens, does not ask you to keep unsafe secrets, and can get help.`],understanding:[`Telling to keep someone safe is responsible, not tattling.`],skills:[`Demonstrate responsibility for personal safety.`],warmUp:`Sort: Safe / Uncomfortable / Unsafe. You sort “an adult asking you to keep a special secret about touching” as unsafe.`,teachTitle:`Personal safety plan`,teachSteps:[`Safe: you can learn, play, and rest without harm.`,`Uncomfortable: something feels off. You can say no, leave, and tell an adult even if you are not sure.`,`Unsafe: harm or a body/privacy boundary being broken. Tell a trusted adult the same day. You will not be in trouble for telling.`,`Trusted adult: listens, does not ask you to keep unsafe secrets, can get help. Name more than one, including a school adult.`,`If someone is hurt now: get an adult. Do not try to be the physical hero.`],story:`The class sorted cards: Safe, Uncomfortable, Unsafe. Cole put an adult asking you to keep a special secret about touching under Unsafe without debating it. That is a tell-an-adult-the-same-day situation. You will not be in trouble for telling.

Cole’s personal safety card stayed private. He named three trusted adults: his classroom teacher every day, someone in the office before school and at lunch, and his aunt who picks him up on Thursdays. At least one school adult.

Uncomfortable is when something feels off but you are not sure — a joke that goes too far, a dare to hide in the bathroom. You can say no, leave, and tell an adult even if you are not sure. If someone is hurt now, get an adult. Do not try to be the physical hero. Telling to keep someone safe is responsible, not tattling.`,activity:`Personal safety card. Private. Circulate quietly.`,worksheetTitle:`Personal safety card`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`adults`,columns:[`Name / role`,`When I could find them`],rows:3},{kind:`text`,id:`uncom`,label:`An uncomfortable (not unsafe) situation`},{kind:`text`,id:`unsure`,label:`If I am not sure it is unsafe, I will`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why is “keep this special secret” a warning sign when a body or safety boundary is involved?`}],sample:{adults:`Classroom teacher — every school day. | Office adult — before school, recess, lunch. | Aunt on Thursdays — after school, or by phone with a parent.`,uncom:`A joke that goes too far, or a dare to hide in the bathroom. It feels off, but I am not sure it is harm.`,unsure:`Say no, leave, and tell an adult even if I am not sure.`,exit:`Unsafe secrets about bodies or touching are a tell-an-adult-the-same-day situation. A trusted adult does not ask you to keep those.`},checks:[`At least one school adult named?`,`Uncomfortable vs unsafe distinguished?`],tip:`If a student discloses a safety concern: stop, thank them, follow EIPS reporting. Do not promise secrecy. Do not investigate. Inform the principal.`,preview:`Next week: group safety and responsible leadership.`},{week:16,unitId:`safety`,title:`Group Safety and Responsible Leadership`,focus:`Looking after more than yourself`,outcomeFocus:`Group safety includes looking after others, following protocols, and using leadership as responsibility — not as power.`,materials:[`Week 16 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Group safety includes looking after others in a group and following protocols.`,`Leadership in safety is noticing, reminding, and getting an adult — not bossing.`],understanding:[`A group's well-being depends on people taking responsibility for more than themselves.`],skills:[`Demonstrate responsibility for group safety.`],warmUp:`Field-trip picture: one student lags to tie a shoe. Who is responsible besides that student? List three roles.`,teachTitle:`Leadership is a safety job`,teachSteps:[`In a group, your choices affect people who did not make the choice.`,`Responsible leadership: notice, remind kindly, get an adult if the reminder is not enough.`,`Not responsible: mocking, leaving someone, making a dangerous dare to look brave.`,`Examples: bus partner missing, someone not wearing a helmet on a class ride, a group chat piling on.`],story:`On a community-league skate, two Grade 5s were captains for the walk over. One captain wanted to be first on the ice. The other noticed a buddy’s helmet strap hanging and a Grade 3 lagging at the door.

Responsible leadership looked like: wait, remind the strap, tell the supervisor the Grade 3 was still in the boot room. Not: yell hurry up, or skate off to look in charge.

The class wrote: looking after more than yourself is the job. Power is not the job.`,activity:`Three group scenarios. For each: who is affected, a responsible leader move, a move that makes it worse.`,worksheetTitle:`Looking after the group`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`group`,columns:[`Situation`,`Who is affected?`,`Responsible leader move`],rowLabels:[`Your bus partner is not on the bus and the driver is closing the door.`,`A group chat is forwarding a photo of a classmate who did not agree.`,`Someone dares the group to sneak past the nut-aware table with snacks.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`worse`,label:`One move that would make any of these worse (and why you will not do it)`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How is safety leadership different from being the boss?`}],sample:{group:`Partner, driver, families — tell the driver and a supervisor now. | The classmate, anyone who sees it, you — stop the share, tell an adult. | Allergic classmate, whole table — refuse the dare, move the snacks, tell a supervisor.`,worse:`Mocking, leaving someone, or joining the dare to look brave. That puts the group at risk to protect my image.`,exit:`A leader notices, reminds, and gets an adult. A boss just wants to be first or in charge.`},checks:[`Did they include getting an adult, not only peer heroics?`,`Did they reject the dare/mock path?`],tip:`Praise the quiet notice. The student who waits at the door is leading.`,preview:`Next week: digital privacy and online responsibility.`},{week:17,unitId:`safety`,title:`Digital Privacy and Online Responsibility`,focus:`Digital citizenship`,outcomeFocus:`Digital privacy includes protecting personal information, images, and messages. Digital choices have real impacts.`,materials:[`Week 17 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Digital privacy includes protecting personal information, images, location, passwords, and messages.`,`Once something is forwarded, you do not control it.`],understanding:[`Digital choices have real impacts on privacy and well-being.`],skills:[`Apply practices that protect digital privacy.`],warmUp:`Sort: share freely / ask first / never post. Start with a photo of a classmate, a home address, a lunch selfie of only you, a password hint.`,teachTitle:`Privacy is a well-being issue`,teachSteps:[`Personal information: full name + school + location together is identifying. Photos of other people need permission.`,`Messages and screenshots: private means private. Forwarding is a choice with impact (Week 13).`,`Location tags, school uniforms in photos, and “guess where I am” games can show strangers where you are.`,`Passwords and codes stay off shared docs and off the bottom of laptops.`,`If something spreads: tell a trusted adult. Do not pile on. Do not investigate by sharing it further.`],story:`A group chat forwarded a screenshot of a private joke. By lunch it was not a joke. Trust was the impact. Repair was delete, apologize privately, tell an adult if it had spread, and not do it again.

The class built a three-bin sort. Share freely: a drawing you made, a score in a school game with no other faces. Ask first: a photo with a classmate in it, a story that names someone. Never: passwords, addresses, body pictures, secrets someone told you.

Digital privacy is responsibility for well-being — yours and other people’s — in a place where a tap travels farther than a hallway.`,activity:`Sort items, then write one personal digital rule they can actually keep.`,worksheetTitle:`Share / ask / never`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sort`,columns:[`Item`,`Share / Ask first / Never`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Photo of a classmate at recess`,`Your school and the time you walk home`,`A drawing you made`,`A screenshot of a private message`,`A password written under your keyboard`]},{kind:`text`,id:`rule`,label:`One digital rule I can keep this month`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does “I was just forwarding it” not cancel the impact?`}],sample:{sort:`Ask first — their face, their permission. | Never — identifies when and where you are alone. | Share — it is yours. | Never / ask an adult — private means private. | Never — anyone at the desk can use it.`,rule:`I will not forward a screenshot of a private message. If I am not sure, I ask the person or an adult.`,exit:`Once it is forwarded, I do not control it. Trust and well-being are already affected. Responsibility includes not doing that.`},checks:[`Ask-first vs never distinguished?`,`Did they name an impact on someone else?`],tip:`Do not ask students to confess what they have already posted. Hypotheticals. If a live incident exists, follow school procedure privately.`,preview:`Next week: consent and refusal — respecting yes and no.`},{week:18,unitId:`safety`,title:`Consent and Refusal — Respecting Yes and No`,focus:`Permission skills`,outcomeFocus:`Consent is permission for something to happen. Refusal is the right to say no. Responsibility includes respecting yes and no. Grade 5: space, photos, games, borrowing, digital sharing — not sexual activity.`,materials:[`Week 18 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Consent is permission for something to happen or agreement to do something.`,`Refusal is the right to say no. Silence or freeze is not a yes.`,`Responsibility includes respecting the acceptance or refusal of consent from another.`],understanding:[`Respecting yes and no protects well-being and trust.`],skills:[`Demonstrate Grade 5-appropriate consent and refusal (space, photos, games, borrowing, digital sharing).`],warmUp:`Four quick scenes: hug, photo, borrowing a hoodie, tagging someone in a post. Students show yes / no / ask first with fingers. No discussion of bodies as a joke.`,teachTitle:`Ask, wait, respect`,teachSteps:[`Ask first for space (hugs, crowding), photos, games that involve touching, borrowing, and posting someone’s image or words.`,`Wait for a clear yes. Silence, maybe, or freeze is not a yes. “I guess” under pressure is not a yes.`,`No can be said with words, a shake of the head, walking away, or “stop.” All of those count.`,`If you hear no: stop. Do not negotiate, tickle harder, or “it was just a joke.”`,`You can refuse. You do not need a long reason. A trusted adult can help if someone will not stop.`,`This lesson is school-appropriate: permission for space, photos, games, borrowing, and digital sharing. Not sexual activity.`],story:`Priya did not want to be in the group photo. Two classmates said come on, don’t be weird. She said no again and stepped out of the frame. Respecting no meant they took the picture without her and did not post a crop that still showed her shoulder with a mean caption.

Later, Dev asked to borrow her markers. She said yes for the period, not to take them home. He returned them. That is consent with a limit.

The class practised a refusal line that is allowed to be short: No thanks. Stop. I don’t want to. They practised a respect line: Okay. Thanks for telling me. Consent is not a trick. It is responsibility for another person’s well-being.`,activity:`Sort scenes into ask first / it’s a yes / it’s a no / tell an adult. Write one refusal line and one respect line.`,worksheetTitle:`Yes, no, ask, tell`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`scenes`,columns:[`Scene`,`Ask first / Yes / No / Tell an adult`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Someone wants to post a photo of you`,`A friend asks to borrow your hoodie for recess and you say yes`,`Someone keeps tickling after you said stop`,`A classmate wants a hug and you do not`,`An adult asks you to keep a special secret about touching`]},{kind:`text`,id:`refuse`,label:`One short refusal line I can use`},{kind:`text`,id:`respect`,label:`One line that shows I heard no`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why is silence not the same as yes?`}],sample:{scenes:`Ask first — their image, their permission. | Yes — clear agreement with a limit (recess). | No, then tell an adult if it continues — stop means stop. | No — you do not have to hug. | Tell an adult the same day — unsafe secret.`,refuse:`No thanks. Stop. I don’t want to.`,respect:`Okay. Thanks for telling me.`,exit:`Silence, freeze, or “I guess” under pressure is not a clear yes. Responsibility is to wait for a real yes or to accept a no.`},checks:[`Stop after no is named as the required move?`,`Unsafe secret sorted to tell an adult?`,`Stayed on Grade 5 contexts (space, photos, games, borrowing, digital)?`],tip:`If jokes start, reset once and continue. Consent lessons stay school-appropriate. Sexual activity is not this unit.`,preview:`Weeks 19–24: Healthy Eating — Food Guide plate, body systems, FNMI food knowledge.`}],r=[{week:19,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Canada’s Food Guide Plate (2019)`,focus:`Half / quarter / quarter`,outcomeFocus:`Use the 2019 plate, not the old rainbow. AHS Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance is a valid resource.`,materials:[`Week 19 worksheet`,`Optional Food Guide plate on projector`],knowledge:[`Canada’s Food Guide 2019 recommends a plate: ½ vegetables and fruits, ¼ whole grain foods, ¼ protein foods; water as the drink of choice.`,`How we eat matters: meals together when we can, cook more often. One meal is not a panic.`],understanding:[`A plate of proportions is easier to picture at one meal than a rainbow of servings.`],skills:[`Evaluate meals using Canada’s Food Guide plate.`],warmUp:`Draw a circle. Cross out the old rainbow kindly. We use 2019 proportions at a meal.`,teachTitle:`Read the plate`,teachSteps:[`Half: vegetables and fruits (fresh, frozen, or canned).`,`One quarter: whole grain foods (oats, brown/wild rice, whole grain pasta or bread).`,`One quarter: protein foods (beans, lentils, nuts, eggs, fish, lean meat, lower-fat dairy, fortified soy). Plant proteins more often.`,`Water is the drink of choice.`,`How we eat matters: meals together when we can, cook more often, no panic about one meal.`,`Do not comment on anyone’s lunch. Access is not equal.`],story:`Leila drew a circle and kindly crossed out the old rainbow she remembered from a poster in the hallway. Canada’s Food Guide in 2019 uses a plate: half vegetables and fruits, one quarter whole grain foods, one quarter protein foods. Water is the drink of choice.

Plate A was a meal she actually eats: whole-grain toast, scrambled egg, apple slices, and water. Not a chef fantasy. The toast was the grain quarter, egg the protein, apple the fruit. She marked partly because the plate was light on vegetables.

Plate B, one realistic change: add leftover carrots or frozen mixed vegetables on the side. Frozen and canned count. Nobody commented on anyone’s lunch kit. Access is not equal. Why a plate of proportions instead of a rainbow of servings? It is easier to picture at one meal, and it puts vegetables and fruits at half without counting cups in the cafeteria.`,activity:`Plate A = a meal they actually eat. Plate B = one realistic change.`,worksheetTitle:`What’s on the plate?`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`2019 plate: ½ vegetables and fruits · ¼ whole grains · ¼ protein. Water to drink.`},{kind:`table`,id:`plateA`,columns:[`Food`,`Veg/fruit, whole grain, protein, water, or other`],rows:4},{kind:`choice`,id:`close`,label:`How close to half / quarter / quarter?`,options:[`Close`,`Partly`,`Not yet`]},{kind:`text`,id:`plateB`,label:`Plate B — one realistic change (not a fantasy chef meal)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why did Canada switch from a rainbow of servings to a plate of proportions?`}],sample:{plateA:`Whole-grain toast — whole grain. Scrambled egg — protein. Apple slices — veg/fruit. Water — water.`,close:`Partly`,plateB:`Add leftover carrots or frozen mixed vegetables on the side. Frozen and canned count.`,exit:`It is easier to picture at one meal, and it puts vegetables and fruits at half without counting cups in the cafeteria.`},checks:[`Can they point to half / quarter / quarter?`,`Frozen/canned treated as valid?`],tip:`Never grade a lunch kit. Praise one small swap they control.`,preview:`Next week: food and water meeting body systems.`},{week:20,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Nutrition, Hydration, and How the Body Uses Food`,focus:`Energy and nourishment`,outcomeFocus:`Food is fuel, building, and repair. Hydration supports every system. Personal factors change needs.`,materials:[`Week 20 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Nutrition provides energy and nourishment to the body.`,`Carbohydrates fuel. Protein builds and repairs. Fats help absorb some vitamins. Fibre helps digestion. Water carries nutrients and cools you.`,`Personal factors influence nutrition needs (activity, growth, allergies, culture).`],understanding:[`Nutrition contributes to well-being — and it is not the only factor. Food is not a moral test.`],skills:[`Examine how nutrition and hydration support the body.`],warmUp:`What does your body do with lunch besides “not be hungry”? Collect: energy, growth, repair, thinking.`,teachTitle:`Food is more than calories`,teachSteps:[`Carbohydrates fuel. Protein builds and repairs. Fats help absorb some vitamins. Fibre helps digestion. Vitamins/minerals are small but essential.`,`Water carries nutrients, cools you, helps blood move. Thirst is a late signal.`,`Needs differ: soccer night vs reading night; growth spurts; allergies; cultural fasting. Those are factors, not “picky.”`,`Food is not a moral test (good kid / bad kid). No calorie counting in Grade 5.`],story:`What does your body do with lunch besides not being hungry? Marcus listed energy, growth, repair, and thinking through math. Food is more than calories.

He matched plate sections to jobs in his own words. Vegetables and fruits: vitamins, fibre, and colour that help the body work. Whole grains: longer-lasting energy. Protein: building and repair after gym. Water: helps blood move and keeps you from fading in the afternoon.

A personal factor that changes needs: soccer night versus a reading night, or an allergy in the class that means we do not share. Those are factors, not picky. Food is not a moral test. No calorie counting. Nutrition contributes a lot to well-being — energy and mood — and it is not the only factor.`,activity:`Match plate sections to jobs; note one personal factor.`,worksheetTitle:`Fuel, fluids, and factors`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`jobs`,columns:[`This…`,`Main job in the body (your words)`],rowLabels:[`Vegetables and fruits`,`Whole grain foods`,`Protein foods`,`Water`]},{kind:`text`,id:`factor`,label:`One personal factor that changes needs`},{kind:`text`,id:`activity`,label:`High-activity day vs low-activity day — food or water`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`To what extent does nutrition contribute to well-being? Two sentences, one example.`}],sample:{jobs:`Veg/fruit: vitamins, fibre, digestion. Grains: longer-lasting energy. Protein: building and repair. Water: carries nutrients, cools you. Thirst is a late signal.`,factor:`Soccer night versus a reading night, or an allergy that means we do not share.`,activity:`High-activity: extra water bottle and a protein-plus-fruit snack. Low-activity: still eat a regular meal; I do not skip just because I sat more.`,exit:`A lot — energy and mood for afternoon math. Example: water plus a grain-and-protein lunch keeps me from fading. Sleep and safety still matter too.`},checks:[`More than “food gives energy”?`,`Water treated as essential?`],tip:`If weight-loss talk starts: redirect to nourishment and patterns, not shrinking bodies.`,preview:`Next week: supporting the heart and digestion.`},{week:21,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Supporting the Heart and Digestion`,focus:`Cardiovascular and digestive systems`,outcomeFocus:`Nutrition and hydration support the cardiovascular and digestive systems. Patterns matter more than one meal.`,materials:[`Week 21 worksheet`],knowledge:[`The digestive system breaks food down. Fibre and water help waste move.`,`The cardiovascular system (heart and vessels) moves oxygen and nutrients.`],understanding:[`Everyday patterns of vegetables, fruit, whole grains, protein, and water support these systems. One birthday cake does not ruin a heart.`],skills:[`Examine how nutrition and hydration support body systems.`],warmUp:`Fist on chest (heart is a muscle). Trace mouth → stomach → intestines.`,teachTitle:`Two systems, one lunch`,teachSteps:[`Digestive: breaks food down. Fibre + water help waste move. Eating slowly helps.`,`Cardiovascular: heart and vessels move oxygen and nutrients. Everyday pattern of veg/fruit, whole grains, protein, water supports it. Sugary drinks and very salty snacks as the usual pattern make the system work harder.`,`One birthday cake does not ruin a heart. Patterns matter.`,`We do not diagnose from a Health lesson. Talk in often versus sometimes.`],story:`Anika made a fist on her chest. The heart is a muscle. She traced mouth to stomach to intestines with a finger in the air. Two systems, one lunch.

They compared snacks. Apple plus a handful of nuts: fibre and protein, helpful over time. Large sugary drink plus chips most days: not a helpful usual pattern. Why does water show up on both lists? It helps digestion move, and it helps blood carry what the heart is pumping. We do not diagnose from a Health lesson. We talk in often versus sometimes.`,activity:`Systems table + two-snack compare.`,worksheetTitle:`Heart, gut, and a snack`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sys`,columns:[`System`,`What it does`,`A habit that supports it`],rowLabels:[`Digestive`,`Cardiovascular (heart and blood)`]},{kind:`table`,id:`snack`,columns:[`Snack`,`Helps digestion?`,`Helps the heart over time?`],rowLabels:[`Apple + handful of nuts`,`Large sugary drink + chips most days after school`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does water show up on both lists?`}],sample:{sys:`Digestive: breaks food down; fibre plus water; eat slowly, include fruit or veg. Cardiovascular: heart and vessels move oxygen and nutrients; everyday plate pattern.`,snack:`Apple + nuts: yes and yes as an often snack. Sugary drink + chips most days: not a helpful usual pattern.`,exit:`Water helps digestion move, and it helps blood carry what the heart is pumping.`},checks:[`Patterns vs one meal named?`,`No diagnosing classmates?`],tip:`Keep language in often / sometimes. Never label a student’s home food as bad.`,preview:`Next week: muscles, bones, and reproductive health — still about nourishment.`},{week:22,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Supporting Muscles, Bones, and Reproductive Health`,focus:`Musculoskeletal and reproductive systems`,outcomeFocus:`Nutrition and hydration also support the musculoskeletal system and a healthy reproductive system as the body matures. Stay on nourishment, not shrinking bodies.`,materials:[`Week 22 worksheet`],knowledge:[`The musculoskeletal system (muscles and bones) needs protein, calcium-rich foods, vitamin D (food + safe daylight), water, and activity.`,`Nutrition supports the reproductive system as it matures during puberty (sleep, food, activity — not special “puberty diets”).`],understanding:[`The same Food Guide pattern supports more than one system.`],skills:[`Examine how nutrition and hydration support body systems.`],warmUp:`Flex a forearm. Tap a shin. Those are muscle and bone. Ask: what did breakfast have to do with gym class?`,teachTitle:`Nourish the frame — and the systems that mature`,teachSteps:[`Muscles use protein for repair after activity. Bones use calcium-rich foods (dairy, fortified plant drinks, canned fish with bones, some greens) plus activity and vitamin D.`,`Water helps muscles work and recover. “I forgot my bottle” is a well-being issue on a soccer day.`,`The reproductive system is also a body system. As it matures in puberty, the same pattern helps: regular meals, iron-rich foods, water, rest. We do not teach special shrinking diets.`,`Iron-rich foods (meat, beans, lentils, fortified cereal, greens) support blood. This matters for growing bodies, including people who menstruate — name it calmly, no scare stories. Details of menstruation return in Week 28.`],story:`After intramurals, Noah’s calves shook. Protein at lunch (beans in chili, or an egg) is repair, not a luxury. Calcium-rich food and actually using the bones in gym support the frame you are growing.

The class added the reproductive system to the systems list without turning it into a giggle-fest. Same plate. Same water. Same sleep. No “eat this to look older.” No comments on anyone’s body. Nourishment supports systems that are maturing. That is the POS connection. How pregnancy happens is not this lesson.`,activity:`Match systems to foods and habits. One respectful sentence about why we do not diet-talk in Grade 5 Health.`,worksheetTitle:`Frame and systems`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sys`,columns:[`System`,`What it needs (food / water / habit)`,`One example I could actually eat or do`],rowLabels:[`Muscles`,`Bones`,`Reproductive system (maturing)`]},{kind:`text`,id:`iron`,label:`One iron-rich food (meat, beans, lentils, fortified cereal, greens) I know`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why do we talk about nourishing a maturing body, not about shrinking a body, in Grade 5?`}],sample:{sys:`Muscles: protein + water after activity — chili beans. Bones: calcium-rich food + gym — fortified soy drink or milk, and actually moving. Reproductive: regular meals, iron-rich foods, sleep, water — the same plate, not a special diet.`,iron:`Lentils in soup, or fortified cereal, or beef in a taco.`,exit:`The outcome is well-being and body systems. Shrinking talk is not Grade 5 PEW and it harms people whose bodies are changing at different speeds.`},checks:[`Muscles and bones connected to food AND activity?`,`Reproductive system named as a system, without pregnancy/birth content?`],tip:`If body-size comments start, shut them down once and return to systems. Growth unit will handle puberty with more care in Weeks 25–30.`,preview:`Next week: cultural, traditional, and medicinal food practices, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit.`},{week:23,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Cultural, Traditional, and Medicinal Food Practices`,focus:`Including FNMI food knowledge`,outcomeFocus:`Cultural, traditional, and medicinal food practices, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit, contribute to well-being. Respect, not a recipe test.`,materials:[`Week 23 worksheet`,`Optional: a locally appropriate, school-approved FNMI resource (not a random internet list)`],knowledge:[`Food practices can be cultural, traditional, and medicinal.`,`First Nations, Métis, and Inuit food knowledge includes relationships with land, water, animals, and plants — not only a grocery list.`,`Valid learning comes from respectful sources: Knowledge Keepers, Elders, school-approved resources — not stereotypes.`],understanding:[`Respect for diverse food practices supports belonging and well-being.`,`Canada’s Food Guide includes First Nations, Inuit, and Métis resources that sit beside the plate, not under it.`],skills:[`Investigate cultural, traditional, and medicinal food practices, including First Nations, Métis, and Inuit.`],warmUp:`Name one food that means something in your family (celebration, comfort, everyday). No ranking. No “weird food” talk.`,teachTitle:`Food is knowledge, not a costume`,teachSteps:[`Many families have cultural food practices: what is eaten, when, with whom, and how it is prepared. These can support belonging and well-being.`,`Traditional practices are passed down. Medicinal practices use foods or plants as part of staying well — this is knowledge, not a Grade 5 diagnosis kit.`,`First Nations, Métis, and Inuit food knowledge in Alberta may include (examples, not a complete list): berries, fish, moose or other hunted foods, bannock, tea, gardens, and teachings about taking only what is needed. Specific nations (Cree, Dene, Blackfoot, Métis, and others) have their own knowledge. Do not flatten them into one “Native food.”`,`Canada’s Food Guide has complementary First Nations, Inuit, and Métis healthy eating resources. The 2019 plate is one tool. It is not the only valid way to eat.`,`We listen with respect. We do not treat sacred or medicinal knowledge as a craft activity. We do not require students to share family food if they do not want to.`],story:`The class made two columns: What I know from my family, and What I am learning with respect. Nobody had to fill the first column out loud.

A guest resource (school-approved) described how some Métis families smoke fish and how some Cree teachings connect berries to a season, not to a supermarket week. The point was relationship with land and people, not “try this at home without context.”

Leila wrote: my family’s Friday rice is cultural. Bannock in a textbook is not mine to claim. I can respect it without pretending it is my tradition. Medicinal does not mean I get to pick a plant on the field trip and experiment. Knowledge Keepers decide what is shared.`,activity:`Respect notes: one family practice (optional), one FNMI idea learned with respect, one “I will not” (stereotype or taking knowledge).`,worksheetTitle:`Food, culture, and respect`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`family`,label:`Optional: one food practice from my family or community (you may write “private”)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`learn`,label:`One First Nations, Métis, or Inuit food teaching I can state respectfully (from class, not from a joke or a movie)`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`plate`,label:`How can traditional foods sit beside the Food Guide plate instead of being ranked as “less healthy”?`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What is the difference between respecting a food practice and copying it as a costume?`}],sample:{family:`Friday rice with the people I live with. Celebration food, not a contest.`,learn:`Some Métis and First Nations families have teachings about fish, berries, and taking only what is needed. Nations have their own knowledge — not one “Native food.”`,plate:`Traditional foods can be vegetables, grains, protein, and water too. The plate is a tool. It does not rank someone’s culture.`,exit:`Respect is listening and not ranking. Copying is taking something that is not mine and using it as a costume or a craft without context or permission.`},checks:[`No stereotype language?`,`FNMI knowledge treated as knowledge, not a recipe test?`],tip:`If you do not have a local Knowledge Keeper, use school-approved CFG / FNMI resources. Do not improvise sacred content. Invite, do not extract.`,preview:`Next week: finding nutritional balance — assessing meals with a valid resource.`},{week:24,unitId:`nutrition`,title:`Finding Nutritional Balance — Assessing Meals`,focus:`Unit wrap · AHS resource`,outcomeFocus:`Assess nutritional balance of meals using valid resources (CFG 2019, AHS Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance). Answer the guiding question.`,materials:[`Week 24 worksheet`,`Optional: AHS Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance (valid support)`,`Unit rubric`],knowledge:[`Valid resources help us assess meals: Canada’s Food Guide 2019, Alberta Health Services Grade 5 Finding Nutritional Balance.`,`Balance is a pattern over time, not a perfect plate at every sitting.`],understanding:[`Nutrition contributes to health and well-being of body systems — to an important extent — alongside sleep, safety, belonging, and activity.`],skills:[`Assess nutritional balance of meals using valid resources.`],warmUp:`Post the guiding question: To what extent does nutrition contribute to health and well-being? Two silent sentences, then two shares.`,teachTitle:`Assess, don’t shame`,teachSteps:[`Use the plate: half / quarter / quarter + water. Frozen and canned count. Cultural foods count.`,`Ask: often or sometimes? One meal is data, not a verdict on a family.`,`Name which systems a balanced pattern supports: digestive, cardiovascular, musculoskeletal, reproductive (maturing).`,`Answer the guiding question with a claim + example + a limit (nutrition is not the only factor).`],story:`Marcus assessed two sample meals, not anyone’s lunch kit. Meal 1: leftover stew with carrots, barley, and beans, water. Close to the plate. Meal 2: a granola bar and a sugary drink on a rushed morning. Sometimes happens. Pattern goal: add fruit and water the next morning, not a shame speech.

To what extent? A lot — energy, mood, systems. Not the only extent — sleep, safety, and belonging still matter. That is a Grade 5 answer with a limit, which is what “to what extent” asks for.`,activity:`Assess two sample meals (provided). Then one meal they eat. Then the guiding-question paragraph. Score the unit rubric once.`,worksheetTitle:`Balance check`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`meals`,columns:[`Meal`,`Close / partly / not yet`,`One realistic change`],rowLabels:[`Stew with carrots, barley, beans + water`,`Granola bar + sugary drink on a rushed morning`]},{kind:`text`,id:`mine`,label:`A meal I actually eat — how close, and one change I control`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`systems`,label:`Name two body systems a balanced pattern supports, in your words`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`To what extent does nutrition contribute to health and well-being? Claim + example + one limit.`}],sample:{meals:`Stew: close — veg, grain, protein, water. | Granola bar + sugary drink: not yet as a usual breakfast; add fruit and water next time, no shame.`,mine:`Toast, egg, apple — partly; add leftover carrots. Frozen counts.`,systems:`Digestive (fibre and water) and cardiovascular (everyday plate pattern, water).`,exit:`A lot — energy and mood for afternoon math, and support for heart, gut, muscles, and a maturing body. Limit: sleep and safety still matter, and one rushed morning is not a verdict.`},checks:[`Guiding question answered with a limit?`,`No lunch-kit shaming?`],tip:`AHS Finding Nutritional Balance is a valid support if your school has it. CFG 2019 plate is enough if not.`,preview:`Weeks 25–30: Growth & Development. Send the family letter at least one month before Week 25.`}],i=[{week:25,unitId:`growth`,title:`Maturation and Changes in Adolescence`,focus:`Physical, cognitive, behavioural`,outcomeFocus:`Adolescence is the stage between childhood and adulthood. Maturation is the process of growing and developing. Changes are physical, cognitive (thinking), and behavioural. Timing varies. No one is “late” as a joke.`,materials:[`Week 25 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Adolescence is the life stage between childhood and adulthood.`,`Maturation is the process of growing and developing.`,`Changes during adolescence are physical, cognitive, and behavioural.`],understanding:[`Everyone’s timeline is different. Comparing bodies in class is not allowed.`],skills:[`Investigate maturation and identify changes during adolescence.`],warmUp:`Three columns: Body / Thinking / Behaviour. Students add one change they have noticed in general (not pointing at a classmate).`,teachTitle:`Adolescence is a range, not a race`,teachSteps:[`Physical: growth spurts, sleep need, appetite, sweating, skin, body shape, hair in new places. These connect to hormones — chemical messengers.`,`Cognitive: longer-term thinking starts to grow; you can still feel very in-the-moment.`,`Behavioural: want more independence, care more what peers think, mood can swing. That is common, not a character flaw.`,`Everyone’s timeline is different. Comparing bodies in class is not allowed.`,`Questions go in the box. We answer facts next class. We do not answer personal questions about anyone in the room.`],story:`Ibrahim did not point at anyone. He added to the columns on the board: Body — needing more sleep; Thinking — caring more about a group project going well; Behaviour — wanting a closed door when doing homework. Adolescence is the stage between childhood and adulthood. Maturation is the process of growing and developing. Timing varies. No one is late as a joke.

Why is it unkind to rank who hit puberty first? Bodies are not a race, and ranking makes people feel watched. Giggles happened. The teacher reset once — calm science voices — and continued. Nobody was shamed for giggling.`,activity:`Sort changes and write one respectful sentence about different timelines.`,worksheetTitle:`Changes during adolescence`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`changes`,columns:[`Change`,`Physical, thinking, or behaviour?`,`A supportive response`],rowLabels:[`Needing more sleep`,`Caring more what friends think`,`Growth spurt / clothes don't fit`,`Wanting a closed bedroom door more often`]},{kind:`text`,id:`rank`,label:`Why is it unkind to rank who “hit puberty” first?`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Name one physical, one thinking, and one behaviour change that can happen in adolescence.`}],sample:{changes:`Sleep — physical — do not tease; growing bodies need rest. | Friends — thinking/behaviour — normal; still use your values. | Clothes — physical — different timelines, no ranking. | Door — behaviour — privacy can be independence; families can still check in.`,rank:`Bodies are not a race. Ranking makes people feel watched. Timing varies. Comparing bodies in class is not allowed.`,exit:`Physical: needing more sleep. Thinking: caring more about a group project. Behaviour: wanting a closed door for homework.`},checks:[`Can they use the three categories?`,`No pointing at classmates?`],tip:`Giggles are normal. Reset once (“we use calm science voices”) and continue. Do not shame.`,preview:`Next week: physical changes of puberty with correct words, calmly.`},{week:26,unitId:`growth`,title:`Physical Changes During Puberty`,focus:`Hormones and secondary characteristics`,outcomeFocus:`Puberty is the set of physical changes that lead toward adult reproductive capacity. Hormones increase. Secondary sex characteristics appear. Timing varies.`,materials:[`Week 26 worksheet`,`Question box`],knowledge:[`Hormones are chemical messengers. The pituitary gland signals ovaries or testicles to make more hormones (estrogen, progesterone, testosterone).`,`Secondary sex characteristics include breast development, voice deepening, hair in new places, wider hips or shoulders, more oil and sweat, growth spurt.`],understanding:[`People notice different mixes and timings. Growth speed does not tell you character or worth.`],skills:[`Identify changes during adolescence connected to puberty and hormones.`],warmUp:`Define hormone in one kid sentence: a chemical message that tells the body to change or do a job.`,teachTitle:`What puberty does`,teachSteps:[`Pituitary gland in the brain signals ovaries or testicles to make more hormones (estrogen, progesterone, testosterone — names on the board, not a memorization test).`,`Primary changes: reproductive organs mature (next two weeks).`,`Secondary characteristics: breast development; voice deepening; hair under arms, on legs, on the face, and in the pubic area; wider hips or wider shoulders; more oil on skin; more sweat; growth spurt.`,`People may notice different mixes and timings. Stick to facts. No commenting on a classmate’s body.`,`Voice cracks, extra laundry, and needing deodorant are normal. Hygiene is respect for yourself and others.`],story:`Hormone, Sophie said: a chemical message that tells the body to change or do a job. Names went on the board: estrogen, progesterone, testosterone — not a memorization test.

The class sorted fact and myth. Everyone starts puberty at the same age: myth. Hormones are chemical messengers: fact. Sweat and skin oil often increase: fact. You can tell everything about a person from how fast they grow: myth.

Secondary characteristics were listed as science, not as a checklist of classmates. Voice cracks, extra laundry, and needing deodorant are normal. Hygiene is respect.`,activity:`Fact / myth table plus two general physical changes (not naming a classmate).`,worksheetTitle:`Puberty facts`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`fm`,columns:[`Statement`,`Fact or myth?`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Everyone starts puberty at the same age.`,`Hormones are chemical messengers.`,`Sweat and skin oil often increase.`,`You can tell everything about a person from how fast they grow.`]},{kind:`text`,id:`two`,label:`Two physical changes I might notice or that a friend might notice (general, not naming a classmate)`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Who is a trusted adult I could ask a puberty question?`}],sample:{fm:`Myth — timing varies. | Fact — they tell the body to change or do a job. | Fact — secondary characteristics can include more oil and sweat. | Myth — growth speed is not character, smarts, or worth.`,two:`A growth spurt that makes clothes short, and needing deodorant because sweat increases. General facts, not a person in this room.`,exit:`A parent or guardian, or my teacher. Questions can also go in the class question box.`},checks:[`Hormones defined simply?`,`Myths about same-age start and judging bodies busted?`],tip:`If a student is visibly anxious, offer to complete the sheet in a quieter space. Same content.`,preview:`Next: organs and what they do — still no pregnancy/birth sequence.`},{week:27,unitId:`growth`,title:`Puberty and Reproductive Capacity`,focus:`Organs and functions`,outcomeFocus:`Each part of the human reproductive system has a job. Puberty matures those parts so the body becomes capable of reproduction in the future. We name parts and functions. The fertilization-through-pregnancy-and-birth sequence is Grade 6.`,materials:[`Week 27 worksheet`,`Simple unlabeled diagrams if your school provides them — or word lists only`],knowledge:[`Each part of the human reproductive system serves a specific function.`,`Typical female system: ovaries store egg cells; uterus is a muscular organ with a lining; vagina is a canal; vulva is the outside parts; fallopian tubes carry an egg toward the uterus.`,`Typical male system: testicles produce sperm cells and testosterone; scrotum holds the testicles; penis delivers urine and, later, semen.`,`The human reproductive system and other body systems are interconnected.`],understanding:[`Having these organs mature means the body is gaining the capacity for human reproduction. Capacity is not the same as being ready to parent, and it is not a request to discuss sexual activity.`],skills:[`Identify the components of the human reproductive system.`,`Describe the functions of the components of the human reproductive system.`],warmUp:`Rules: correct words, calm voice, no jokes about anyone in the room. Pass is allowed.`,teachTitle:`Parts have jobs`,teachSteps:[`Female system (typical): ovaries store egg cells; uterus is a muscular organ with a lining; vagina is a canal from the uterus to the outside; vulva is the outside parts. Fallopian tubes carry an egg toward the uterus — name them. Do not teach the fertilization-to-birth storyline; that sequence is Grade 6.`,`Male system (typical): testicles produce sperm cells and testosterone; scrotum holds the testicles; penis delivers urine and, later, semen.`,`These systems connect to other systems (hormones, blood). Growing them is part of overall maturation.`,`Capacity means the body is becoming able, in the future, to produce egg or sperm cells. It is not the same as being ready to parent.`,`Drawings stay scientific. No phones out. Use correct words: penis, testicles, vulva, vagina, uterus, ovaries.`],story:`Rules on the board: correct words, calm voice, no jokes about anyone in the room. Pass is allowed. Ravi matched organs to jobs in Grade 5 language.

Ovaries store egg cells. The uterus is a muscular organ with a lining. The vagina is a canal from the uterus to the outside; the vulva is the outside parts. Testicles produce sperm cells and testosterone. The scrotum holds the testicles. The penis delivers urine and, later, semen.

Having these organs mature means the body is gaining the capacity for human reproduction. Capacity is not the same as being ready to parent, and it is not a request to discuss sexual activity. They did not walk fertilization, implantation, trimesters, or birth — that sequence is Grade 6. Why correct anatomical words instead of slang? Slang turns the lesson into a joke contest and can humiliate people. Correct words keep it science.`,activity:`Match organ → job. Short “capacity vs ready” sentence.`,worksheetTitle:`Organs and jobs`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`organs`,columns:[`Part`,`Job in your own words`],rowLabels:[`Ovaries`,`Uterus`,`Vagina / vulva`,`Testicles`,`Penis`,`Scrotum`]},{kind:`text`,id:`capacity`,label:`What does “capacity for human reproduction” mean in Grade 5 language?`,rows:2},{kind:`text`,id:`not`,label:`What are we not learning this year (Grade 6 content)?`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why do we use correct anatomical words instead of slang?`}],sample:{organs:`Ovaries: store egg cells. Uterus: muscular organ with a lining. Vagina: canal; vulva: outside parts. Testicles: sperm and testosterone. Penis: urine and, later, semen. Scrotum: holds the testicles.`,capacity:`The body is becoming able, in the future, to produce egg or sperm cells. That is not the same as being ready to parent, and we are not studying sexual activity.`,not:`The fertilization-through-pregnancy-and-birth sequence, contraception, and dating advice. That is Grade 6 and later.`,exit:`Slang turns the lesson into a joke contest and can humiliate people. Correct words keep it science.`},checks:[`Organs matched to jobs with correct words?`,`Capacity distinguished from “ready to parent”?`,`No Grade 6 pregnancy/birth sequence?`],tip:`If your school provides diagrams, keep them scientific and unlabeled until students fill jobs. No phones. Pass is allowed.`,preview:`Next week: menstruation and sperm production as signs of reproductive capacity.`},{week:28,unitId:`growth`,title:`Menstruation and Sperm Production`,focus:`Signs of reproductive capacity`,outcomeFocus:`Menstruation and sperm production are signs that the reproductive system is maturing. Teach the processes factually. Hygiene and products, calmly. Not sexual activity.`,materials:[`Week 28 worksheet`,`Question box`,`Optional: unopened pad (still in wrapper) as a visual — no demo on a body`],knowledge:[`Menstruation is a monthly cycle that prepares the uterus; if an egg is not fertilized, the lining leaves the body as a period.`,`Ovulation is when an ovary releases an egg. Hormones regulate the cycle.`,`Testicles produce sperm. Sperm travels through the vas deferens and is combined with other fluid to produce semen.`,`These processes are signs of reproductive capacity — not a signal to start sexual activity, which is not taught in Grade 5.`],understanding:[`Physical changes during puberty affect reproductive capability. Timing varies. Periods and sperm production can start at different ages. First periods can be irregular.`],skills:[`Describe how physical changes during puberty affect reproductive capability.`,`Describe the processes of menstruation and sperm production.`],warmUp:`Reset the science-voice rule. Today’s words: menstruation, period, ovulation, sperm, semen, hygiene. Giggles get one reset.`,teachTitle:`Two signs of capacity`,teachSteps:[`Menstruation: hormones help the uterus grow a lining. An ovary may release an egg (ovulation). If that egg is not going to become a pregnancy, the lining leaves the body through the vagina — that is a period. Blood and tissue. It can last several days. First periods are often irregular. This is a sign the system is maturing.`,`People who menstruate can use a pad (sits in underwear), a tampon (goes in the vagina — name it, do not practise), or other products families choose. Change them, wash hands, and tell a trusted adult if pain is intense or if they are unprepared at school. Schools have a plan for that — know yours.`,`Sperm production: testicles make sperm cells. Sperm travels through the vas deferens and mixes with fluid to make semen. Erections and wet dreams can happen and are common. They are not something to joke about in the hallway.`,`We name that pregnancy can become possible later when sperm and egg meet. How that happens, implantation, and birth are Grade 6. Grade 5 stops at capacity and the two signs.`,`Everyone in the room learns both systems. This is science, not a boys’ lesson and a girls’ lesson.`],story:`The class used calm science voices. Menstruation is a cycle. A period is the lining leaving the body. It is not dirty and it is not a punishment. Sophie wrote: if I am unprepared at school I go to the office or a trusted adult. Pads are in more than one place so nobody has to announce it.

Ravi wrote: sperm is made in the testicles and semen is sperm plus fluid. Wet dreams can happen. You wash and you do not perform them as a joke.

Why does everyone learn both? Because these are human body systems, classmates deserve dignity, and you may need to be a respectful friend or a future trusted adult. Capacity is not a green light. It is biology.`,activity:`Two-column process notes + hygiene + “everyone learns both.” Private questions go in the box.`,worksheetTitle:`Two signs of capacity`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`proc`,columns:[`Process`,`What happens, in Grade 5 language`],rowLabels:[`Menstruation / a period`,`Ovulation (one line)`,`Sperm production / semen`]},{kind:`text`,id:`hygiene`,label:`One hygiene or help step (pad, wash, trusted adult, extra clothes) a person might need`},{kind:`text`,id:`both`,label:`Why does everyone in Grade 5 learn both systems?`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What does “sign of reproductive capacity” mean — and what does it not mean?`}],sample:{proc:`Period: uterus lining leaves the body through the vagina for a few days; first ones can be irregular. | Ovulation: an ovary releases an egg. | Testicles make sperm; sperm plus fluid is semen.`,hygiene:`A pad in underwear, wash hands, extra clothes in a bag, tell a trusted adult or the office if I am unprepared or in a lot of pain.`,both:`These are human body systems. Classmates deserve dignity. You may need to be a respectful friend.`,exit:`It means the system is maturing so pregnancy could become possible later. It does not mean someone is ready to parent, and it is not a lesson on sexual activity.`},checks:[`Both processes stated without slang?`,`Hygiene named without embarrassment theatre?`,`Grade 6 sequence left in Grade 6?`],tip:`Know where pads are in your building before this lesson. Answer question-box items factually next class. Skip anything that is not Grade 5 PEW.`,preview:`Next week: positive health practices during puberty.`},{week:29,unitId:`growth`,title:`Positive Health Practices During Puberty`,focus:`Sleep, food, hygiene, activity, adults`,outcomeFocus:`Positive health practices during puberty include physical activity, nutritious foods, reducing stress, sleep, hygiene, and talking to trusted adults. These support a healthy reproductive system and overall well-being.`,materials:[`Week 29 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Positive health practices during puberty include engaging in physical activity, eating nutritious foods, reducing stress, sleep, hygiene, and connecting with trusted adults.`],understanding:[`Positive health practices during puberty support a healthy reproductive system and well-being.`],skills:[`Identify positive health practices during puberty.`],warmUp:`List five practices on the board: sleep, food, activity, hygiene, trusted adult. Students add reducing stress as a sixth (a walk, a quiet corner, telling someone — not “just calm down”).`,teachTitle:`Practices, not perfection`,teachSteps:[`Sleep: growing bodies often need more. Phones out of the bed if the family can.`,`Food: the same Food Guide pattern. Iron-rich foods matter for some. No shrinking diets.`,`Activity: daily movement supports mood, bones, and the systems we named.`,`Hygiene: deodorant, washing, clean clothes, period products. This is respect, not a popularity contest.`,`Stress: puberty can feel loud. Reducing stress is a health practice — break, talk, move, rest.`,`Trusted adults: questions and worries go to a person, not to a random comment section.`],story:`Sophie’s list was ordinary: earlier bedtime than she wanted, water bottle, deodorant in the backpack, a walk after a loud day, and her aunt as a second adult besides a parent. Nothing fancy.

The class crossed out “look older” and “look smaller” as health practices. Those are not PEW. Positive health practices support a body that is maturing. They do not rank it.`,activity:`Build a personal practice card — six small, realistic items. No sharing of private hygiene details out loud.`,worksheetTitle:`Practices that support a maturing body`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`pract`,columns:[`Practice`,`What this could look like for me this month`],rowLabels:[`Sleep`,`Nutritious food / water`,`Physical activity`,`Hygiene`,`Reducing stress`,`Trusted adult`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How do positive health practices support a healthy reproductive system — in one kid sentence?`}],sample:{pract:`Sleep: phone out of bed when I can. Food: water + a protein-and-fruit snack after gym. Activity: intramurals twice a week. Hygiene: deodorant in the backpack. Stress: a walk after a loud day. Adult: parent and my teacher.`,exit:`Sleep, food, movement, hygiene, and less stress help the whole body, including the systems that are maturing, work the way they are supposed to.`},checks:[`Six practices attempted?`,`No body-ranking language?`],tip:`Some students cannot control bedtime or food at home. Keep items in the student’s circle of control. School water and school movement count.`,preview:`Next week: connecting puberty to well-being — unit wrap.`},{week:30,unitId:`growth`,title:`Connecting Puberty to Well-Being`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Pull both outcomes together: investigate maturation, and connect puberty to reproductive capacity — with respect, accurate language, and health practices. Still no Grade 6 sequence.`,materials:[`Week 30 worksheet`,`Unit rubric`,`Question box (last pass)`],knowledge:[`Well-being during adolescence is supported by accurate knowledge, respectful participation, and positive health practices.`],understanding:[`Knowledge of maturation and reproductive capacity supports well-being when it is factual and respectful.`],skills:[`Investigate maturation and identify changes during adolescence.`,`Connect puberty to the capacity for human reproduction.`],warmUp:`Three sentences on the board to finish: Puberty is… Capacity means… I will not…`,teachTitle:`What we taught — and what we did not`,teachSteps:[`We taught: maturation; physical, cognitive, and behavioural changes; puberty and hormones; secondary sex characteristics; reproductive organs and functions; menstruation and sperm production as signs of capacity; positive health practices.`,`We did not teach: the fertilization-through-pregnancy-and-birth sequence (Grade 6); sexual activity; contraception; dating advice.`,`Gender identity lessons are not part of this unit plan — stay on the PEW growth outcomes named above.`,`Pass was allowed. Question box was for facts, not for personal stories about people in the room.`],story:`Ravi finished: Puberty is a set of physical changes toward adult reproductive capacity. Capacity means the body can, later, produce egg or sperm cells — not that someone is ready to parent. I will not rank bodies or use slang about classmates.

Sophie added a health-practice line: sleep, food, activity, hygiene, a trusted adult. That is connecting puberty to well-being. The science was the content. The dignity was the method.`,activity:`Unit wrap paragraph + self-check against the rubric. Remaining question-box items answered factually if they are Grade 5 PEW.`,worksheetTitle:`Growth wrap`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`puberty`,label:`Puberty is… (one accurate sentence)`},{kind:`text`,id:`capacity`,label:`Capacity for human reproduction means… (and does not mean…)`},{kind:`text`,id:`change`,label:`One physical, one thinking, and one behaviour change`},{kind:`text`,id:`practice`,label:`Two positive health practices I can keep`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How does accurate, respectful knowledge about puberty support well-being?`}],sample:{puberty:`Puberty is the set of physical changes, driven by hormones, that lead toward adult reproductive capacity. Timing varies.`,capacity:`It means the body is becoming able to produce egg or sperm cells. It does not mean someone is ready to parent, and it is not a lesson on sexual activity or birth.`,change:`Physical: growth spurt. Thinking: caring more about a group project. Behaviour: wanting more privacy.`,practice:`Water bottle and deodorant in the bag; talk to a trusted adult instead of a comment section.`,exit:`Facts replace rumours. Respect replaces ranking. People can ask for help. That supports well-being during a stage that already feels loud.`},checks:[`Accurate language?`,`Capacity vs ready?`,`No Grade 6 content sneaking in?`],tip:`Score the growth rubric once. Keep returned family-letter slips in a folder. Students who opted out should already have been on the alternate task — never a public list.`,preview:`Weeks 31–36: Financial Literacy — needs, wants, saving, budgets, choices. Sample dollars only.`}],a=[{week:31,unitId:`financial`,title:`Needs and Wants`,focus:`What money is for`,outcomeFocus:`Needs are required for well-being (food, shelter, clothing, safety). Wants are extra. Both are human. Planning starts by telling them apart.`,materials:[`Week 31 worksheet`],knowledge:[`A need supports health and safety. A want is nice-to-have.`,`Context matters: running shoes can be a need for gym and a want if they must be a $250 brand.`],understanding:[`Telling needs from wants is the first planning skill. Families have different needs. We do not rank anyone’s house, lunch, or phone.`],skills:[`Distinguish needs and wants as a foundation for financial planning.`],warmUp:`Sort: water, video game, winter coat, extra hoodie, bus fare, concert merch. Two disagreements — talk them out.`,teachTitle:`Needs, wants, and grey zones`,teachSteps:[`A need supports health and safety. A want is nice-to-have.`,`Context matters: running shoes can be a need for gym and a want if they must be a $250 brand.`,`Families have different needs. We do not rank anyone’s house, lunch, or phone.`,`Telling them apart is the first planning skill. You can still enjoy a want — after the needs are covered, or with a plan.`],story:`The class sorted: water, video game, winter coat, extra hoodie, bus fare, concert merch. Hana and Jack disagreed about the extra hoodie. Context: a winter coat can be a need. A second hoodie with a ninety-dollar logo is often a want. Running shoes can be a need for gym and a want if they must be a two-hundred-fifty-dollar brand.

Needs support health and safety: lunch, a helmet, a charger if the family phone is how you reach a parent. Wants are extra: limited-edition cards, movie snacks. Both are human. Sample dollars only. No one had to say what their family earns.`,activity:`Sort a list, then label one personal example of each without sharing money amounts out loud.`,worksheetTitle:`Need or want?`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`sort`,columns:[`Item`,`Need, want, or depends?`,`Why`],rowLabels:[`Lunch for school`,`Limited-edition cards`,`Helmet for biking`,`Brand-name hoodie`,`Phone charger`,`Movie snacks`]},{kind:`text`,id:`need`,label:`One need in my life`},{kind:`text`,id:`want`,label:`One want I can wait for`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does mixing up needs and wants make planning harder?`}],sample:{sort:`Lunch: need. Cards: want. Helmet: need (safety). Brand hoodie: depends (clothing vs logo). Charger: depends (reach a parent vs extra). Movie snacks: want.`,need:`Winter boots that still fit.`,want:`A new pencil case with a character on it.`,exit:`The want can empty the jar before the boots are covered. You can still enjoy a want after needs are covered, or with a plan.`},checks:[`Can they explain a “depends” item?`,`No shaming of what others have?`],tip:`Some students have almost no spending money. Use school examples (choosing a free activity vs a paid one) so everyone can play.`,preview:`Next: saving and goals — short-term and long-term.`},{week:32,unitId:`financial`,title:`Saving and Financial Goals`,focus:`Short-term and long-term`,outcomeFocus:`Saving means not spending now so money is there for a goal or an unexpected expense. Short-term goals can support long-term ones.`,materials:[`Week 32 worksheet`,`Calculator optional`],knowledge:[`Short-term goals: days to a few months. Long-term goals: a year or more.`,`Saving strategies: name the number and the time, skip some wants, put money aside on purpose (jar, envelope, account with a family adult).`],understanding:[`A short-term save helps a long-term goal because the habit is the same skill.`],skills:[`Set short-term and long-term financial goals and plan a simple save-up.`],warmUp:`Goal: $40 soccer registration in 8 weeks. How much per week if they save evenly? ($5) What if they also want a $10 snack this weekend?`,teachTitle:`Goals need a number and a time`,teachSteps:[`Short-term: days to a few months (a book, a field trip fee, a gift).`,`Long-term: a year or more, usually more money, needs commitment (a bike, a camp, helping family with a bigger item).`,`Saving strategies: decide the goal, skip some wants, put money aside on purpose (jar, envelope, account with a family adult).`,`Unexpected expenses are why a small buffer helps. You will not predict everything.`],story:`Goal on the board: forty dollars for soccer registration in eight weeks. Even saving is five dollars a week. Mason also wanted a ten-dollar snack this weekend. The class could see the collision.

Mason’s sample short-term goal: sixteen dollars for a used chapter-book set in four weeks, four dollars a week. Long-term: sixty dollars toward a used helmet and lights in twelve weeks, five dollars a week. One want to pause: extra vending popcorn. A short-term save helps a long-term goal because the habit — putting money aside on purpose — is the same skill, just a smaller number first.`,activity:`Write one short-term and one long-term goal with a simple save-up math box. Keep dollar amounts modest and fictional if needed.`,worksheetTitle:`Two goals, one saving plan`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`goals`,columns:[``,`Short-term goal`,`Long-term goal`],rowLabels:[`What I would save for`,`About how much ($)`,`How many weeks`,`Money to set aside each week`]},{kind:`text`,id:`pause`,label:`One want I would pause to hit the short-term goal`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How can a short-term save (habit) help a long-term goal?`}],sample:{goals:`Short: used chapter-book set, $16, 4 weeks, $4/week. Long: used helmet and lights, $60, 12 weeks, $5/week.`,pause:`Extra vending popcorn this weekend.`,exit:`The habit — putting money aside on purpose — is the same skill. A smaller number first makes the bigger goal more possible.`},checks:[`Both goals have a time and a rough amount?`,`Division attempted (even if you help)?`],tip:`If math stalls the 12 minutes, give the weekly amount and have them check it. The Health idea is the plan, not long division.`,preview:`Next week we put this into a simple budget.`},{week:33,unitId:`financial`,title:`Simple Budgets`,focus:`Income, expenses, leftover`,outcomeFocus:`A budget is a plan for earning, spending, saving, and donating over a period. Saving is a choice in the plan, not a leftover that maybe happens.`,materials:[`Week 33 worksheet`],knowledge:[`A budget includes money on hand or coming in, money going out, and money set aside.`,`Needs first, then wants. If expenses beat income, cut a want or add time to the goal.`],understanding:[`Planning supports financial goals when the numbers actually balance.`],skills:[`Build a simple budget that includes needs, wants, and saving.`],warmUp:`Show a 3-line budget on the board: In $20 (birthday + chores). Needs $8. Wants $6. Save $6. Ask: is this a plan or a wish?`,teachTitle:`Build a tiny budget`,teachSteps:[`Income: gift, chore pay, allowance — whatever is real for that student, or use the class sample numbers. Never a required family income.`,`Expenses: needs first, then wants.`,`Saving and donating are choices in the plan, not leftovers that “maybe” happen.`,`If expenses beat income, cut a want or add time to the goal. Do not pretend.`],story:`Aaliyah copied the three-line budget: in twenty dollars, needs eight, wants six, save six. Is this a plan or a wish? A plan, if she actually follows it.

Sample: thirty dollars for a month of fun plus save. Goal: twelve dollars for a class t-shirt. Needs, a bus and snack fund, ten dollars. Left for wants: eight dollars. She checked that it balanced: thirty equals ten plus eight plus twelve. If it did not balance, she would cut a want, not pretend. The Health idea is the plan, not long division.`,activity:`Budget using the sample numbers on the sheet. Then one personal or sample weekly budget.`,worksheetTitle:`A simple budget`,fields:[{kind:`intro`,text:`Sample: You have $30 for a month of “fun + save.” Goal: $12 saved for a class t-shirt. Needs (bus/snack fund) $10. How much is left for wants?`},{kind:`table`,id:`budget`,columns:[`Budget part`,`Amount ($)`,`Need, want, save, or donate?`],rowLabels:[`Money I have or will get`,`Need 1`,`Want 1`,`Save`,`Donate (optional)`]},{kind:`choice`,id:`balance`,label:`Does it balance (in = out + save)?`,options:[`Yes`,`Not yet — I would change a want`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`What is a budget, in one sentence?`}],sample:{budget:`In $30. Need $10 (bus/snack). Want $8. Save $12 (t-shirt). Donate $0. 30 = 10+8+12.`,balance:`Yes`,exit:`A budget is a plan for money coming in and going out — including saving on purpose, not as a leftover.`},checks:[`Needs before wants?`,`Saving is a line, not an accident?`],tip:`Always offer sample dollars. Never require a real family income.`,preview:`Next week: consumer choices — what influences us.`},{week:34,unitId:`financial`,title:`Consumer Choices — What Influences Us`,focus:`Ads, friends, price, quality`,outcomeFocus:`Examine factors that influence consumer choice: advertising, peers, price, quality, packaging, and feelings. Planning includes noticing those factors.`,materials:[`Week 34 worksheet`,`Optional: one printed ad with the brand name covered`],knowledge:[`Consumer choices are influenced by ads, friends, price, quality, packaging, habit, and feelings (boredom, belonging).`,`A want can be dressed up as a need by an ad.`],understanding:[`Noticing influence is part of planning. You can still buy a want — with a plan, not on autopilot.`],skills:[`Examine factors that influence consumer choice.`],warmUp:`Show an ad (or describe one): bright colours, a kid laughing, “limited time.” Ask: what is the product doing, and what is the ad doing?`,teachTitle:`Influence is not the same as a need`,teachSteps:[`Price: what it costs. Quality: how long it lasts or how well it works. They are not always the same.`,`Ads: colour, music, famous people, countdown timers. Their job is to get a yes.`,`Peers: belonging can feel like a purchase. That is a real feeling. It is still a want until you check.`,`Packaging and placement (eye-level, checkout) are designed. You can notice them.`,`A pause question: Do I need this, want this, or was I just asked very loudly?`],story:`Aaliyah wanted the limited-edition cards because two people at her table had them. The ad said last chance. Price was eight dollars. Quality: they would live in a drawer by next month.

She listed factors: peers (belonging), ad (urgency), price (eight dollars that was also her snack-and-save money). She still wanted them. She decided to wait one week and check the budget line. Waiting was the plan. Not a moral victory speech. If the want was still there and the needs were covered, she could buy them on purpose.

Noticing the influence did not make her a sucker. It made her a planner.`,activity:`Take one real or sample want. Name the influences. Decide: buy now, wait, or skip — and why.`,worksheetTitle:`What is pulling on this choice?`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`item`,label:`One item (sample is fine — do not name what a classmate owns)`},{kind:`checks`,id:`factors`,label:`Influences I notice (check all that apply)`,options:[`Advertising / “limited time”`,`Friends or belonging`,`Price (it seems cheap or it seems expensive)`,`Quality / how long it lasts`,`Packaging or being at checkout`,`Feeling (bored, stressed, left out)`]},{kind:`text`,id:`needwant`,label:`Is this a need, a want, or a want dressed up as a need? Why?`},{kind:`choice`,id:`decide`,label:`My plan`,options:[`Buy now (needs covered, in the budget)`,`Wait one week`,`Skip`]},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why does noticing an influence help a financial goal?`}],sample:{item:`Limited-edition cards, $8, “last chance” ad.`,factors:`Advertising / “limited time”; Friends or belonging; Price (it seems cheap or it seems expensive)`,needwant:`A want dressed up as a need by belonging and a countdown. I do not need them to be well.`,decide:`Wait one week`,exit:`If I buy on autopilot, the want can empty the jar before the goal. Noticing lets me choose on purpose.`},checks:[`More than one influence named?`,`Decision connected to a plan, not to shame?`],tip:`Do not roast anyone’s shoes or lunch. Use sample products.`,preview:`Next week: earn, spend, save, donate — four money jobs.`},{week:35,unitId:`financial`,title:`Planning: Earn, Spend, Save, Donate`,focus:`Four money jobs`,outcomeFocus:`Planning can include four jobs for money: earn, spend, save, donate. Not every student will do all four this month. The skill is to put them in a plan on purpose.`,materials:[`Week 35 worksheet`],knowledge:[`Earning for Grade 5 might be chores, gifts, a small paid task with a family adult — never required work that replaces rest or school.`,`Spending includes needs and wants. Saving is for goals and surprises. Donating is a choice to contribute (connects to Week 1 volunteering — time is also a donation).`],understanding:[`A plan that names all four jobs (even if donate is $0 this month) is more complete than a plan that only spends.`],skills:[`Demonstrate how planning can support financial goals.`],warmUp:`Four corners: earn, spend, save, donate. Students walk to the job they think about most. Then to the job they think about least.`,teachTitle:`Four jobs, one plan`,teachSteps:[`Earn: money in. Keep examples Grade 5 and optional. Gifts count. So does a sample number.`,`Spend: needs first, then planned wants.`,`Save: the goal from Week 32, plus a tiny buffer if possible.`,`Donate: money, or time/talent/energy (Week 1). Both are contribution. Nobody is a better person for a bigger number.`,`If a student has $0, they can still plan with sample dollars and they can still donate time.`],story:`Mason’s sample week: earn $5 from a family task, spend $2 on a planned want (not the vending popcorn), save $3 toward the helmet, donate time by helping the lost-and-found for twenty minutes. Donate money was $0 this week. That still counted as a plan with four jobs, because time was on the donate line.

Hana had no earning this month. She used the class sample $10, spent $4 on a need (bus), saved $6, and donated $0 plus a recycling-crew shift. Planning supported the goal without pretending she had a job.`,activity:`Fill four jobs for a sample week. At least one line must be real-or-sample save. Donate may be time.`,worksheetTitle:`Four jobs for money (and time)`,fields:[{kind:`table`,id:`jobs`,columns:[`Job`,`What this looks like this month (sample dollars are fine)`],rowLabels:[`Earn`,`Spend (need)`,`Spend (want, if any)`,`Save`,`Donate (money or time)`]},{kind:`text`,id:`link`,label:`How does this plan support one financial goal I named in Week 32 or 33?`,rows:2},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`Why can donating time count in a financial-literacy plan in Grade 5?`}],sample:{jobs:`Earn: sample $5 family task. Spend need: $2 bus. Spend want: $0 this week. Save: $3 helmet. Donate: 20 minutes lost-and-found (time).`,link:`The $3 a week is the habit toward the $60 helmet. The want is paused. Time-donate keeps contribution in the plan even at $0.`,exit:`Not every student has money to give. Time, talent, and energy are contribution (Week 1) and they belong in a well-being plan.`},checks:[`Save is a line?`,`Donate allowed to be time?`,`No required family income?`],tip:`If a student is proud of a large donate number from a family, accept it without turning it into a class contest.`,preview:`Last week: my financial plan — unit wrap.`},{week:36,unitId:`financial`,title:`My Financial Plan`,focus:`Unit wrap`,outcomeFocus:`Students demonstrate how planning can support financial goals. One page: needs/wants, a goal, a budget, one influence they will notice, four jobs.`,materials:[`Week 36 worksheet`,`Unit rubric`],knowledge:[`Planning supports financial goals when needs and wants are named, a goal has a number and a time, a budget balances, and influences are noticed.`],understanding:[`Informed financial decision making contributes to well-being — with sample dollars, without ranking families.`],skills:[`Demonstrate how planning can support financial goals.`],warmUp:`One-minute list: need, want, short-term goal, one influence ads use. Four words on paper. Then build the plan around them.`,teachTitle:`The plan is the outcome`,teachSteps:[`A proficient wrap has: a need vs a want, a goal with $ and weeks, a budget that balances on sample or real small numbers, one consumer influence, and a next step.`,`Well-being connection: less worry, a goal that moves, contribution if they chose it — not “being rich.”`,`Score the unit rubric once. Proficient is the target.`],story:`Aaliyah’s one-page plan used sample dollars. Need: bus. Want: cards, waiting one week. Goal: $12 t-shirt in four weeks, $3 a week. Budget: $20 in, $8 need, $0 want this month, $12 save. Influence she will notice: “last chance” ads. Donate: recycling crew.

How does planning support the goal? The number has a time. The want is paused on purpose. The ad is named so it does not empty the jar by accident. That is the outcome in kid language.`,activity:`One-page plan. Optional share of the goal line only — no family money out loud.`,worksheetTitle:`My Grade 5 financial plan`,fields:[{kind:`text`,id:`need`,label:`One need I will cover first`},{kind:`text`,id:`want`,label:`One want I can wait for (or plan for)`},{kind:`text`,id:`goal`,label:`One goal: what, about how much, how many weeks, weekly save`},{kind:`text`,id:`budget`,label:`A tiny budget that balances (in = needs + wants + save + donate)`},{kind:`text`,id:`influence`,label:`One influence I will notice before I buy`},{kind:`exit`,id:`exit`,prompt:`How does planning support a financial goal — in one sentence?`}],sample:{need:`Bus / snack fund.`,want:`Limited-edition cards — wait one week.`,goal:`Class t-shirt, $12, 4 weeks, $3 a week.`,budget:`In $20. Need $8. Want $0 this month. Save $12. Donate $0 + recycling time. Balances.`,influence:`“Last chance” ads and belonging at the table.`,exit:`A goal with a number and a time, needs covered first, and wants chosen on purpose — so the jar is not empty by accident.`},checks:[`Goal has a number and a time?`,`Budget attempted?`,`No family-income sharing?`],tip:`Celebrate the plan, not the dollar size. A $4 field-trip fee is as valid as a $60 helmet.`,preview:`Year complete. Six units, thirty-six lessons, one 30-minute block at a time.`}],o=[...e,...t,...n,...r,...i,...a],s=Object.fromEntries(o.map(e=>[e.week,e]));function c(e){return o.filter(t=>t.unitId===e)}export{s as n,c as r,o as t};